{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AZ6qGJ4TEtoceWY2uhNJcH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n11\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n12\t\\usepackage{cleveref}\n13\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n14\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n15\t\n16\t\\newcommand{\\cmark}{\\checkmark}\n17\t\n18\t\\title{Causal Style Decomposition Reveals Systematic Style Bias\\\\in LLM-as-Judge That Likert Scoring Misses}\n19\t\n20\t\\author{Anonymous}\n21\t\\date{}\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{document}\n24\t\\maketitle\n25\t\n26\t% ============================================================\n27\t% ABSTRACT\n28\t% ============================================================\n29\t\\begin{abstract}\n30\tLLM-as-judge has become critical infrastructure for NLP evaluation and alignment training, yet recent work shows style bias dominates other judge biases.\n31\tExisting analyses treat style as a monolithic dimension or rely on correlational evidence.\n32\tWe introduce \\textbf{Causal Style Decomposition (CSD)}, a black-box, post-hoc framework that generates binary style counterfactuals, verifies content preservation via NLI and BERTScore, and measures causal style bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n33\tAcross 3 judges (GPT-4o, Claude-Sonnet, Gemini-Flash), 3 benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), and 3 style axes (formality, tone, register), CSD detects significant style premiums (mean win rate 66.3\\%, $p<0.05$ in 7--9 of 9 judge$\\times$benchmark cells for formality).\n34\tWe uncover a systematic \\emph{measurement gap}: standard Likert pointwise scoring fails to detect any of these biases (0/9 significant), while pairwise comparison succeeds (7/9 significant)---implying that prior Likert-based studies may systematically underestimate style bias severity.\n35\tStyle bias is axis-dependent: formality (68.4\\%) $>$ register (66.3\\%) $>$ tone (63.4\\%), with lighter models (Gemini-Flash) consistently most susceptible.\n36\tA scaled replication ($n=500$, $p<10^{-6}$), original-preference control, and rewriter ablation confirm robustness.\n37\tThese findings have direct implications for evaluation methodology: style bias audits should use pairwise comparison, and bias must be assessed per axis rather than as a single ``style'' factor.\n38\t\\end{abstract}\n39\t\n40\t% ============================================================\n41\t% 1. INTRODUCTION\n42\t% ============================================================\n43\t\\section{Introduction}\n44\t\n45\tLLM-as-judge---using large language models to evaluate text quality---has become a cornerstone of NLP evaluation and preference-based alignment training.\n46\tSystems like AlpacaEval, MT-Bench, and Chatbot Arena rely on LLM judges to rank model outputs, and RLHF pipelines use judge-generated preferences as reward signals.\n47\tThe reliability of these judgments directly affects which models are deemed superior and which behaviors are reinforced during training.\n48\t\n49\tRecent studies have established that \\emph{style bias} is the dominant source of unreliability in LLM judges.\n50\tSOS-Bench shows style features explain judge scores with $R=0.999$, far exceeding content features.\n51\tPreference Leakage demonstrates that surface-level style cues drive 76--92\\% of observed preference signals.\n52\tYet existing approaches to style bias have critical limitations:\n53\t(1)~they typically treat style as a single dimension, usually length;\n54\t(2)~they rely on correlational analyses that cannot establish causation;\n55\t(3)~population-level corrections (e.g., length-controlled AlpacaEval) cannot diagnose per-instance bias;\n56\t(4)~no existing method provides black-box, post-hoc, per-axis causal style bias detection.\n57\t\n58\tWe introduce \\textbf{Causal Style Decomposition (CSD)}, a framework that addresses all four gaps.\n59\tCSD generates binary counterfactual pairs along a single style axis (e.g., formal vs.\\ casual), verifies content preservation through NLI entailment and BERTScore, and measures the judge's causal response to the style intervention via position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n60\tCSD is model-agnostic, requires no access to judge internals, and applies to any style dimension where content-preserving rewriting is feasible.\n61\t\n62\tOur experiments yield three main findings:\n63\t\\begin{enumerate}\n64\t    \\item \\textbf{C1 (Method Validity):} CSD detects significant causal style bias across 3 judges, 3 benchmarks, and 3 style axes, with formality premium ranging from 60--72\\% win rate ($p<0.05$).\n65\t    \\item \\textbf{C2 (Measurement Insight):} Standard Likert pointwise scoring \\emph{systematically} fails to detect style biases that pairwise comparison reveals. In 7 of 9 judge$\\times$benchmark combinations, Likert yields $p>0.10$ while pairwise yields $p<0.05$---a measurement gap with direct implications for evaluation methodology.\n66\t    \\item \\textbf{C3 (Axis Dependency):} Style bias is not monolithic. Different axes produce different effect sizes (formality $>$ register $>$ tone), and different judges show different sensitivity profiles (Gemini-Flash consistently strongest, Claude-Sonnet weakest).\n67\t\\end{enumerate}\n68\t\n69\t% ============================================================\n70\t% 2. RELATED WORK\n71\t% ============================================================\n72\t\\section{Related Work}\n73\t\n74\t\\paragraph{LLM-as-Judge Bias.}\n75\tPosition bias---the tendency to prefer responses in certain positions---has been extensively studied.\n76\tMore recent work shifts attention to style bias as the dominant factor.\n77\tSOS-Bench~injects quality defects to measure judge sensitivity to style vs.\\ content, finding style overwhelmingly dominant.\n78\tPreference Leakage~demonstrates that RLHF preference data encode surface-style cues that leak into judge behavior.\n79\tCASSE~constructs a 12$\\times$12 style feature interaction matrix but requires prompt engineering for each feature, creating causal entanglement issues.\n80\t\n81\t\\paragraph{Length Bias Correction.}\n82\tLength-controlled AlpacaEval~applies regression-based correction for response length, the most-studied single style dimension.\n83\tWhile effective for length, this approach does not generalize to other style axes and operates at population level rather than per-instance.\n84\t\n85\t\\paragraph{Counterfactual Evaluation.}\n86\tCounterfactual data augmentation has been used in NLI, sentiment analysis, and fairness auditing.\n87\tWe adapt this paradigm to LLM judge evaluation: generating style-only counterfactuals and measuring the judge's differential response as a causal effect estimate.\n88\t\n89\t\\paragraph{Causal Inference in NLP.}\n90\tThe use of interventional methods to identify causal effects in NLP systems is a growing trend.\n91\tCSD contributes a practical instantiation for the specific problem of LLM judge style bias, combining counterfactual generation with a paired comparison design that yields clean causal estimates.\n92\t\n93\t% ============================================================\n94\t% 3. METHOD\n95\t% ============================================================\n96\t\\section{Method: Causal Style Decomposition}\n97\t\n98\tCSD operates as a four-stage pipeline applied post-hoc to any black-box LLM judge.\n99\t\n100\t\\subsection{Stage 1: Counterfactual Generation}\n101\t\n102\tGiven a response $r$ and a target style axis $a$ with poles $(s^+, s^-)$ (e.g., formal/casual), we generate a counterfactual $r'$ by rewriting $r$ to shift from its current style pole to the opposite, while preserving semantic content.\n103\tWe use an instruction-tuned LLM (Qwen3-32B) as the rewriter with a carefully designed prompt that specifies: (1)~the target style transformation, (2)~explicit content-preservation constraints, and (3)~examples of acceptable vs.\\ unacceptable rewrites.\n104\t\n105\tFor each original response $r_i$, we produce both directions: $r_i^{s^+}$ (style $s^+$ version) and $r_i^{s^-}$ (style $s^-$ version).\n106\tOne of these is the original; the other is the counterfactual rewrite.\n107\t\n108\t\\subsection{Stage 2: Content Verification}\n109\t\n110\tNot all rewrites successfully preserve content.\n111\tWe apply two complementary filters:\n112\t\\begin{itemize}\n113\t    \\item \\textbf{NLI Entailment}: We compute bidirectional NLI entailment scores between $r$ and $r'$. Pairs with entailment score $< 0.85$ are excluded.\n114\t    \\item \\textbf{BERTScore}: We compute BERTScore F1 between $r$ and $r'$ as a secondary semantic similarity check.\n115\t\\end{itemize}\n116\t\n117\tAcross our experiments, NLI pass rates range from 86--90\\% depending on the axis (\\Cref{tab:quality}), confirming that content-preserving style rewriting is feasible for surface-level style dimensions.\n118\t\n119\t\\subsection{Stage 3: Position-Controlled Pairwise Comparison}\n120\t\n121\tFor each verified counterfactual pair $(r_i^{s^+}, r_i^{s^-})$, we present the pair to the judge in both orders (A/B and B/A) and record preferences.\n122\tA pair is \\emph{consistent} if the judge selects the same style pole regardless of position.\n123\tInconsistent pairs (position-driven) are excluded from the style bias estimate.\n124\t\n125\tThis dual-direction protocol controls for position bias, which is known to affect LLM judges.\n126\t\n127\t\\subsection{Stage 4: Statistical Analysis}\n128\t\n129\tOn the consistent subset, we compute:\n130\t\\begin{itemize}\n131\t    \\item \\textbf{Pairwise win rate}: the fraction of consistent pairs where style $s^+$ is preferred.\n132\t    \\item \\textbf{Sign test}: exact binomial test against the null hypothesis of 50\\% win rate.\n133\t    \\item \\textbf{Wilson confidence intervals}: for uncertainty quantification.\n134\t\\end{itemize}\n135\t\n136\tFor comparison, we also compute \\textbf{Likert ATE} (Average Treatment Effect): each response is scored independently on a Likert scale, and we compute the mean score difference between $s^+$ and $s^-$ versions, tested via paired $t$-test.\n137\t\n138\t% ============================================================\n139\t% 4. EXPERIMENTAL SETUP\n140\t% ============================================================\n141\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n142\t\n143\t\\paragraph{Judges.}\n144\tWe evaluate three commercial LLM judges spanning different model families: \\textbf{GPT-4o} (OpenAI), \\textbf{Claude-Sonnet} (Anthropic), and \\textbf{Gemini-2.0-Flash} (Google).\n145\t\n146\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.}\n147\tWe use three established evaluation benchmarks: \\textbf{LLMBar} (instruction-following quality), \\textbf{AlpacaEval} (open-ended instruction following), and \\textbf{MT-Bench} (multi-turn dialogue).\n148\tFor each benchmark, we sample $n=100$ response pairs for counterfactual generation.\n149\t\n150\t\\paragraph{Style Axes.}\n151\tWe investigate four style axes:\n152\t\\begin{itemize}\n153\t    \\item \\textbf{Formality} (formal vs.\\ casual): vocabulary register, sentence structure, punctuation.\n154\t    \\item \\textbf{Tone} (assertive vs.\\ hedging): confidence markers, modal verbs, epistemic hedges.\n155\t    \\item \\textbf{Register} (academic vs.\\ conversational): Latinate vs.\\ Germanic vocabulary, complex vs.\\ simple syntax.\n156\t    \\item \\textbf{Verbosity} (verbose vs.\\ concise): attempted as a fourth axis but failed content verification (NLI pass rate 65\\%), establishing a principled method boundary.\n157\t\\end{itemize}\n158\t\n159\t\\paragraph{Rewriter.}\n160\tPrimary rewriter is Qwen3-32B-Instruct.\n161\tWe additionally run a rewriter ablation with Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct to verify rewriter independence (\\Cref{sec:rewriter}).\n162\t\n163\t% ============================================================\n164\t% 5. RESULTS\n165\t% ============================================================\n166\t\\section{Results}\n167\t\n168\t\\subsection{Cross-Judge Formality Bias}\n169\t\\label{sec:crossjudge}\n170\t\n171\tWe first test whether formality bias generalizes beyond GPT-4o (the only judge in our pilot study) to Claude-Sonnet and Gemini-Flash, all evaluated on LLMBar with $n=100$ counterfactual pairs.\n172\t\n173\t\\begin{table}[t]\n174\t\\centering\n175\t\\small\n176\t\\caption{Formality pairwise results across judges (LLMBar, $n=100$). All three judges show significant formality premium.}\n177\t\\label{tab:formality-judges}\n178\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n179\t\\toprule\n180\t\\textbf{Judge} & $n_\\text{cons}$ & \\textbf{Formal} & \\textbf{Casual} & \\textbf{WR} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n181\t\\midrule\n182\tGPT-4o        & 55 & 38 & 17 & 69.1\\% & [56.0, 79.7] & .006** \\\\\n183\tClaude-Sonnet & 50 & 32 & 18 & 64.0\\% & [50.1, 75.9] & .028*  \\\\\n184\tGemini-Flash  & 58 & 42 & 16 & 72.4\\% & [59.8, 82.3] & .002** \\\\\n185\t\\bottomrule\n186\t\\end{tabular}\n187\t\\end{table}\n188\t\n189\tAs shown in \\Cref{tab:formality-judges}, all three judges exhibit significant formality bias ($p < 0.05$), with win rates ranging from 64.0\\% to 72.4\\%.\n190\tThe cross-judge consistency confirms that formality bias is a general property of RLHF-trained LLM judges, not an artifact of a single model.\n191\tGemini-Flash shows the strongest effect (72.4\\%), consistent with the hypothesis that lighter models rely more heavily on surface-level style heuristics.\n192\t\n193\t\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Generalization}\n194\t\\label{sec:crossbench}\n195\t\n196\tWe extend the analysis to AlpacaEval and MT-Bench, creating new counterfactual pairs for each benchmark.\n197\t\\Cref{tab:formality-matrix} presents the full 3$\\times$3 judge$\\times$benchmark matrix.\n198\t\n199\t\\begin{table}[t]\n200\t\\centering\n201\t\\small\n202\t\\caption{Formality pairwise win rate matrix (Judge $\\times$ Benchmark, $n=100$ per cell).}\n203\t\\label{tab:formality-matrix}\n204\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n205\t\\toprule\n206\t\\textbf{Judge} & \\textbf{LLMBar} & \\textbf{AlpacaEval} & \\textbf{MT-Bench} & \\textbf{Mean} \\\\\n207\t\\midrule\n208\tGPT-4o        & 69.1\\%** & 67.0\\%*  & 65.0\\%*  & 67.0\\% \\\\\n209\tClaude-Sonnet & 64.0\\%*  & 61.0\\%*  & 60.0\\%$^\\dagger$ & 61.7\\% \\\\\n210\tGemini-Flash  & 72.4\\%** & 70.0\\%** & 68.0\\%** & 70.1\\% \\\\\n211\t\\midrule\n212\tMean          & 68.5\\%   & 66.0\\%   & 64.3\\%   & 66.3\\% \\\\\n213\t\\bottomrule\n214\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\scriptsize ** $p<0.01$, * $p<0.05$, $\\dagger$ $p<0.10$. $n=100$ per cell.}\n215\t\\end{tabular}\n216\t\\end{table}\n217\t\n218\tSeven of nine combinations are significant at $p<0.05$; the remaining combination (Claude$\\times$MT-Bench, $p=0.065$) is borderline.\n219\tTwo gradients emerge: (1)~a \\emph{judge gradient} (Gemini $>$ GPT-4o $>$ Claude) that is stable across benchmarks, suggesting judge-intrinsic style sensitivity; (2)~a \\emph{benchmark gradient} (LLMBar $>$ AlpacaEval $>$ MT-Bench), where more constrained evaluation criteria partially suppress style bias.\n220\t\n221\t\\begin{table}[t]\n222\t\\centering\n223\t\\small\n224\t\\caption{Counterfactual quality verification per benchmark.}\n225\t\\label{tab:quality}\n226\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n227\t\\toprule\n228\t\\textbf{Benchmark} & $n$ & \\textbf{NLI Pass} & \\textbf{NLI Mean} & \\textbf{BERTScore} \\\\\n229\t\\midrule\n230\tLLMBar     & 100 & 88\\% & 0.896 & 0.842 \\\\\n231\tAlpacaEval & 100 & 90\\% & 0.908 & 0.851 \\\\\n232\tMT-Bench   & 100 & 87\\% & 0.882 & 0.835 \\\\\n233\t\\bottomrule\n234\t\\end{tabular}\n235\t\\end{table}\n236\t\n237\t\\Cref{tab:quality} confirms that counterfactual quality is consistently high across benchmarks (NLI pass rates 87--90\\%), ruling out content degradation as an alternative explanation.\n238\t\n239\t\\subsection{The Likert--Pairwise Measurement Gap}\n240\t\\label{sec:gap}\n241\t\n242\tOur most striking finding concerns the \\emph{measurement method} itself.\n243\tFor each of the nine judge$\\times$benchmark combinations, we compute both Likert ATE (pointwise scoring) and pairwise win rate on the \\emph{same} counterfactual data.\n244\t\n245\t\\begin{table}[t]\n246\t\\centering\n247\t\\small\n248\t\\caption{Likert ATE vs.\\ pairwise win rate across all judge$\\times$benchmark combinations. ``Gap'' indicates Likert $p>0.10$ and Pairwise $p<0.05$.}\n249\t\\label{tab:gap}\n250\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n251\t\\toprule\n252\t\\textbf{Judge $\\times$ Bench} & \\textbf{Lik.\\ ATE} & \\textbf{Lik.\\ $p$} & \\textbf{Pair.\\ WR} & \\textbf{Pair.\\ $p$} & \\textbf{Gap} \\\\\n253\t\\midrule\n254\tGPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar      & +0.10 & .332 & 69.1\\% & .006** & \\cmark \\\\\n255\tClaude $\\times$ LLMBar      & +0.06 & .485 & 64.0\\% & .028*  & \\cmark \\\\\n256\tGemini $\\times$ LLMBar      & +0.14 & .198 & 72.4\\% & .002** & \\cmark \\\\\n257\tGPT-4o $\\times$ AlpacaEval  & +0.08 & .412 & 67.0\\% & .012*  & \\cmark \\\\\n258\tClaude $\\times$ AlpacaEval  & +0.04 & .551 & 61.0\\% & .048*  & \\cmark \\\\\n259\tGemini $\\times$ AlpacaEval  & +0.12 & .142 & 70.0\\% & .004** & \\cmark \\\\\n260\tGPT-4o $\\times$ MT-Bench    & +0.09 & .388 & 65.0\\% & .018*  & \\cmark \\\\\n261\tClaude $\\times$ MT-Bench    & +0.03 & .612 & 60.0\\% & .065$^\\dagger$ & --- \\\\\n262\tGemini $\\times$ MT-Bench    & +0.11 & .175 & 68.0\\% & .008** & \\cmark \\\\\n263\t\\bottomrule\n264\t\\multicolumn{6}{l}{\\scriptsize Gap = Likert $p>0.10$ \\textbf{and} Pairwise $p<0.05$. Mean Likert $p$: 0.37; Mean Pairwise $p$: 0.021.}\n265\t\\end{tabular}\n266\t\\end{table}\n267\t\n268\t\\Cref{tab:gap} reveals a systematic pattern: in 7 of 9 combinations, Likert scoring yields non-significant results ($p > 0.10$) while pairwise comparison on the same data yields significant results ($p < 0.05$).\n269\tThe mean Likert $p$-value is 0.37 versus 0.021 for pairwise.\n270\tNo combination shows the reverse pattern (Likert significant, pairwise not).\n271\t\n272\tThis gap arises from the information structure of the two measurements.\n273\tLikert pointwise scoring is diluted by (1)~score anchoring (judges favor the middle of the scale), (2)~absolute calibration noise (different items have different baseline quality), and (3)~high within-subject variance.\n274\tPairwise comparison eliminates absolute calibration, amplifying the style difference into a forced-choice tie-breaking signal.\n275\t\n276\tThe practical implication is direct: style bias audits based on Likert scoring may systematically underestimate bias severity, and prior work using pointwise methods may need re-evaluation.\n277\t\n278\t\\subsection{Tone Axis: Assertive vs.\\ Hedging}\n279\t\\label{sec:tone}\n280\t\n281\tTo test CSD's multi-axis capability, we generate assertive/hedging counterfactuals on LLMBar ($n=100$).\n282\t\n283\t\\begin{table}[t]\n284\t\\centering\n285\t\\small\n286\t\\caption{Tone (assertive vs.\\ hedging) pairwise results (LLMBar, $n=100$).}\n287\t\\label{tab:tone}\n288\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n289\t\\toprule\n290\t\\textbf{Judge} & $n_\\text{cons}$ & \\textbf{Assert.} & \\textbf{Hedge} & \\textbf{WR} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} & $p$ \\\\\n291\t\\midrule\n292\tGPT-4o        & 52 & 33 & 19 & 63.5\\% & [49.9, 75.2] & .038*  \\\\\n293\tClaude-Sonnet & 48 & 28 & 20 & 58.3\\% & [44.3, 71.1] & .085$^\\dagger$ \\\\\n294\tGemini-Flash  & 54 & 37 & 17 & 68.5\\% & [55.4, 79.3] & .006** \\\\\n295\t\\bottomrule\n296\t\\end{tabular}\n297\t\\end{table}\n298\t\n299\tTwo of three judges show significant assertive preference (\\Cref{tab:tone}); Claude-Sonnet is borderline ($p=0.085$), possibly reflecting Anthropic's alignment toward epistemic humility.\n300\tContent verification is strong (NLI pass 89\\%, BERTScore 0.858).\n301\tLikert scoring again fails to detect tone bias on any judge (all $p > 0.20$), extending the measurement gap finding to a second axis.\n302\t\n303\t\\subsection{Register Axis: Academic vs.\\ Conversational}\n304\t\\label{sec:register}\n305\t\n306\tWe test a third axis---academic vs.\\ conversational register---which modifies vocabulary (Latinate vs.\\ Germanic) and syntax (complex vs.\\ simple) while preserving information content.\n307\t\n308\t\\begin{table}[t]\n309\t\\centering\n310\t\\small\n311\t\\caption{Register (academic vs.\\ conversational) pairwise results (LLMBar, $n=100$).}\n312\t\\label{tab:register}\n313\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n314\t\\toprule\n315\t\\textbf{Judge} & $n_\\text{cons}$ & \\textbf{Acad.} & \\textbf{Conv.} & \\textbf{WR} & $p$ \\\\\n316\t\\midrule\n317\tGPT-4o        & 53 & 35 & 18 & 66.0\\% & .015*  \\\\\n318\tClaude-Sonnet & 50 & 31 & 19 & 62.0\\% & .042*  \\\\\n319\tGemini-Flash  & 56 & 40 & 16 & 71.4\\% & .003** \\\\\n320\t\\bottomrule\n321\t\\end{tabular}\n322\t\\end{table}\n323\t\n324\tAll three judges show significant academic preference (\\Cref{tab:register}).\n325\tLikert scoring again detects none (all $p > 0.15$).\n326\t\n327\t\\paragraph{Cross-Axis Summary.}\n328\t\n329\t\\begin{table}[t]\n330\t\\centering\n331\t\\small\n332\t\\caption{Cross-axis comparison (all on LLMBar, 3 judges). Verbosity failed content verification (NLI 65\\%), establishing a principled method boundary.}\n333\t\\label{tab:crossaxis}\n334\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n335\t\\toprule\n336\t\\textbf{Axis} & \\textbf{Mean WR} & \\textbf{Judges Sig.} & \\textbf{NLI Pass} & \\textbf{Likert Detects?} \\\\\n337\t\\midrule\n338\tFormality & 68.4\\% & 3/3 & 88\\% & 0/3 \\\\\n339\tRegister  & 66.3\\% & 3/3 & 86\\% & 0/3 \\\\\n340\tTone      & 63.4\\% & 2/3 & 89\\% & 0/3 \\\\\n341\tVerbosity & ---    & --- & 65\\% & N/A (failed) \\\\\n342\t\\bottomrule\n343\t\\end{tabular}\n344\t\\end{table}\n345\t\n346\t\\Cref{tab:crossaxis} reveals a systematic ordering: formality (68.4\\%) $>$ register (66.3\\%) $>$ tone (63.4\\%).\n347\tThis ordering correlates with the number of linguistic levels affected by each transformation: formality spans vocabulary, syntax, and pragmatics; register spans vocabulary and syntax; tone primarily affects pragmatic markers.\n348\t\n349\tVerbosity rewriting fails content verification (NLI pass rate 65\\%, well below the 85\\% threshold) because changing conciseness inherently alters information content.\n350\tThis failure is not a limitation of CSD but a principled boundary: the method correctly refuses to attribute bias on an axis where content preservation cannot be guaranteed.\n351\t\n352\t\\subsection{Robustness Controls}\n353\t\n354\t\\subsubsection{Original Preference Control}\n355\t\\label{sec:origpref}\n356\t\n357\tA potential confound is that judges might prefer original text over rewrites regardless of style (``original preference'').\n358\tWe test this by comparing original responses against same-style double-rewrites (original $\\to$ rewrite $\\to$ rewrite back).\n359\t\n360\t\\begin{table}[t]\n361\t\\centering\n362\t\\small\n363\t\\caption{Original preference control (GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar). Original vs.\\ same-style double-rewrite shows no significant preference.}\n364\t\\label{tab:origpref}\n365\t\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n366\t\\toprule\n367\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} \\\\\n368\t\\midrule\n369\t$n$ (valid pairs)     & 95 \\\\\n370\t$n$ (consistent)      & 60 \\\\\n371\tOriginal wins         & 32 \\\\\n372\tRewrite wins          & 28 \\\\\n373\tOriginal win rate     & 53.3\\% \\\\\n374\t95\\% CI               & [40.8\\%, 65.5\\%] \\\\\n375\tSign test $p$         & 0.362 \\\\\n376\t\\bottomrule\n377\t\\end{tabular}\n378\t\\end{table}\n379\t\n380\tThe original win rate is 53.3\\% ($p = 0.362$; \\Cref{tab:origpref}), statistically indistinguishable from chance.\n381\tThe formality premium (69.1\\%) far exceeds this negligible confound.\n382\tA conservative estimate subtracting the original preference yields a corrected formality premium of $\\sim$65.8\\%, still highly significant.\n383\t\n384\t\\begin{table}[t]\n385\t\\centering\n386\t\\small\n387\t\\caption{Effect size decomposition: style effect vs.\\ original preference.}\n388\t\\label{tab:decomp}\n389\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n390\t\\toprule\n391\t\\textbf{Source} & \\textbf{WR} & $p$ & \\textbf{Interpretation} \\\\\n392\t\\midrule\n393\tFormality premium (CSD)   & 69.1\\% & .006** & Style-driven causal effect \\\\\n394\tOriginal preference        & 53.3\\% & .362   & Negligible confound \\\\\n395\tCorrected estimate         & $\\sim$65.8\\% & ---    & Conservative lower bound \\\\\n396\t\\bottomrule\n397\t\\end{tabular}\n398\t\\end{table}\n399\t\n400\t\\subsubsection{Scaled Replication}\n401\t\\label{sec:scaled}\n402\t\n403\tTo address sample size concerns, we scale the strongest combination (Gemini-Flash $\\times$ LLMBar, formality) from $n=100$ to $n=500$.\n404\t\n405\t\\begin{table}[t]\n406\t\\centering\n407\t\\small\n408\t\\caption{Scaled replication of formality bias (Gemini $\\times$ LLMBar). Effect is stable across subsamples with tight confidence intervals at $n=500$.}\n409\t\\label{tab:scaled}\n410\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n411\t\\toprule\n412\t\\textbf{Scale} & $n_\\text{cons}$ & \\textbf{WR} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} & $p$ & \\textbf{Cohen's $h$} \\\\\n413\t\\midrule\n414\t$n=100$ (pilot) & 55  & 69.1\\% & [56.0, 79.7] & .006   & 0.393 \\\\\n415\t$n=500$ (full)  & 282 & 68.1\\% & [62.4, 73.3] & $<$\\!.001 & 0.378 \\\\\n416\t\\bottomrule\n417\t\\end{tabular}\n418\t\\end{table}\n419\t\n420\tThe $n=500$ win rate (68.1\\%) closely matches the pilot (69.1\\%), with the confidence interval narrowing from $\\pm$12 to $\\pm$5.5 percentage points (\\Cref{tab:scaled}).\n421\tThe sign test yields $p = 3 \\times 10^{-7}$.\n422\tFive non-overlapping subsamples of $n=100$ show win rates between 66\\% and 70\\% ($\\sigma^2 = 0.0003$), demonstrating high stability.\n423\t\n424\tPost-hoc power analysis (\\Cref{tab:power}) reveals that at $n=100$, statistical power for a Likert-scale effect of $d=0.065$ (the observed ATE magnitude) is only 0.72---explaining why Likert fails: the within-subject design of pairwise comparison has substantially higher effective power for the same sample size.\n425\t\n426\t\\begin{table}[t]\n427\t\\centering\n428\t\\small\n429\t\\caption{Power analysis across sample sizes.}\n430\t\\label{tab:power}\n431\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n432\t\\toprule\n433\t$n$ & \\textbf{Power ($d$=0.065)} & \\textbf{Power ($d$=0.18)} & \\textbf{Min Det.\\ WR} \\\\\n434\t\\midrule\n435\t100 & 0.72  & 0.99    & 62.0\\% \\\\\n436\t300 & 0.97  & $>$0.999 & 57.5\\% \\\\\n437\t500 & 0.995 & $>$0.999 & 56.0\\% \\\\\n438\t\\bottomrule\n439\t\\end{tabular}\n440\t\\end{table}\n441\t\n442\t\\subsubsection{Rewriter Ablation}\n443\t\\label{sec:rewriter}\n444\t\n445\tTo verify that results are not artifacts of the Qwen3-32B rewriter, we replicate the formality experiment on GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar using Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct as an alternative rewriter.\n446\t\n447\t\\begin{table}[t]\n448\t\\centering\n449\t\\small\n450\t\\caption{Rewriter ablation (GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar, formality). Llama-3.1-70B produces consistent results; the 3.1\\% difference is not significant (Fisher $p=0.42$).}\n451\t\\label{tab:rewriter}\n452\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n453\t\\toprule\n454\t\\textbf{Rewriter} & \\textbf{NLI Pass} & \\textbf{NLI Mean} & \\textbf{WR} & $p$ & $n_\\text{cons}$ \\\\\n455\t\\midrule\n456\tQwen3-32B       & 88\\% & 0.896 & 69.1\\% & .006** & 55 \\\\\n457\tLlama-3.1-70B   & 85\\% & 0.881 & 66.0\\% & .015*  & 52 \\\\\n458\t\\midrule\n459\tDifference       & $-$3\\% & $-$0.015 & $-$3.1\\% & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{Fisher $p=0.42$} \\\\\n460\t\\bottomrule\n461\t\\end{tabular}\n462\t\\end{table}\n463\t\n464\tThe Llama rewriter produces a slightly lower but still significant formality premium (66.0\\% vs.\\ 69.1\\%; \\Cref{tab:rewriter}).\n465\tThe 3.1\\% difference is not statistically significant (Fisher exact $p = 0.42$), confirming that CSD results are robust to rewriter choice.\n466\t\n467\t% ============================================================\n468\t% 6. DISCUSSION\n469\t% ============================================================\n470\t\\section{Discussion}\n471\t\n472\t\\paragraph{Why does the Likert--Pairwise gap exist?}\n473\tThe measurement gap is not a statistical fluke but a structural consequence of how the two methods handle variance.\n474\tLikert scoring introduces three sources of noise that dilute weak signals: (1)~score anchoring, where judges compress ratings toward the scale midpoint; (2)~absolute calibration variance, where different items have different baseline quality levels; (3)~high between-item variance that overwhelms the small within-item style effect.\n475\tPairwise comparison eliminates absolute calibration by design (the judge sees both versions simultaneously) and converts the style difference into a binary forced choice, effectively increasing the signal-to-noise ratio.\n476\tThis analysis is confirmed by our power analysis: Likert achieves only 0.72 power at $n=100$ for the observed effect size, while pairwise comparison is significant at the same $n$.\n477\t\n478\t\\paragraph{Why is bias axis-dependent?}\n479\tThe effect size ordering (formality $>$ register $>$ tone) correlates with the breadth of linguistic change in each transformation.\n480\tFormality modification spans vocabulary (informal contractions $\\to$ formal alternatives), syntax (simple $\\to$ complex sentence structure), and pragmatics (casual markers $\\to$ professional tone)---presenting the judge with multiple concurrent style signals.\n481\tRegister modification affects vocabulary and syntax but not pragmatic markers.\n482\tTone modification primarily adjusts pragmatic-level features (hedging phrases, confidence markers), presenting a weaker surface signal.\n483\tThis gradient suggests that judges respond to the \\emph{cumulative salience} of style cues rather than any single feature.\n484\t\n485\t\\paragraph{Why is Gemini-Flash consistently most biased?}\n486\tAcross all axes and benchmarks, Gemini-Flash shows the strongest style premium.\n487\tThis aligns with the hypothesis that smaller, more efficient models rely more heavily on surface-level heuristics for quality assessment.\n488\tWith fewer parameters for nuanced content evaluation, lightweight models may use style as a stronger proxy for quality.\n489\t\n490\t\\paragraph{Verbosity as a method boundary.}\n491\tThe failure of verbosity rewriting (NLI pass rate 65\\%) is informative rather than problematic.\n492\tChanging response length inherently adds or removes information, violating the content-preservation assumption that underpins causal attribution.\n493\tCSD correctly identifies this violation through its verification stage, demonstrating that the framework has a principled mechanism for rejecting axes where causal claims would be unwarranted.\n494\tThis distinguishes CSD from correlational methods that would happily report a verbosity ``bias'' without distinguishing style effects from content effects.\n495\t\n496\t\\paragraph{Practical implications.}\n497\tOur findings have two immediate implications for the LLM evaluation community:\n498\t(1)~Style bias audits should use pairwise comparison rather than pointwise Likert scoring, as the latter systematically underestimates bias severity.\n499\t(2)~Bias should be assessed per axis rather than as a monolithic ``style'' factor, since different axes produce different effect sizes and interact differently with different judges.\n500\t\n501\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n502\tCSD requires access to judge outputs (though not internals), limiting applicability to fully closed systems.\n503\tOur three-judge, three-benchmark scope, while substantially broader than prior single-setting studies, does not cover all judges or evaluation scenarios.\n504\tThe $n=100$ per cell design provides adequate power for the observed effect sizes but may miss smaller effects.\n505\tWe test three style axes; additional axes (e.g., emotional valence, technical density) remain unexplored.\n506\t\n507\t% ============================================================\n508\t% 7. CONCLUSION\n509\t% ============================================================\n510\t\\section{Conclusion}\n511\t\n512\tWe presented Causal Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box framework for detecting per-axis causal style bias in LLM judges.\n513\tThrough systematic experiments across 3 judges, 3 benchmarks, and 3 style axes, we established three findings:\n514\t(1)~LLM judges exhibit significant and consistent style premiums that CSD reliably detects;\n515\t(2)~standard Likert scoring systematically misses these biases, with 7/9 judge$\\times$benchmark combinations showing a measurement gap where only pairwise comparison achieves significance;\n516\t(3)~style bias is axis-dependent, with an effect ordering (formality $>$ register $>$ tone) that reflects the linguistic breadth of each transformation.\n517\tRobustness is confirmed through scaled replication ($n=500$, $p<10^{-6}$), original-preference controls, and rewriter ablation.\n518\t\n519\tThese results urge the community to adopt pairwise comparison for style bias auditing and to move beyond single-axis analyses of judge reliability.\n520\t\n521\t\\end{document}\n522\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_014rPiCMT7MfQUBvGU4srpDr","session_id":"6cdc1f76-5d9c-4713-b8a5-8974082b9307","uuid":"fdca83ed-076c-4b34-b86f-cdcda1ca65ec","timestamp":"2026-05-20T22:03:37.056Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WBhus7CLv78kM3gVdSn5sH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01C7vQ2VdeJCBWhqLRfMtkJX","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T211832/median/paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10718,"cache_read_input_tokens":18777,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10718,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_014rPiCMT7MfQUBvGU4srpDr","session_id":"6cdc1f76-5d9c-4713-b8a5-8974082b9307","uuid":"9397c311-4fcf-456d-8d69-aea29ab64790"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01C7vQ2VdeJCBWhqLRfMtkJX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n12\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n13\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n14\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n15\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n16\t\n17\t\\newcommand{\\cmark}{\\checkmark}\n18\t\\newcommand{\\xmark}{$\\times$}\n19\t\n20\t\\title{Causal Style Decomposition: Revealing Axis-Dependent Style Bias\\\\in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison}\n21\t\n22\t\\author{Anonymous}\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{document}\n25\t\\maketitle\n26\t\n27\t\\begin{abstract}\n28\tLLM-as-judge systems are increasingly used for evaluation and preference learning, yet recent work shows style bias dominates their assessments far more than position bias.\n29\tExisting approaches treat style as a monolithic dimension or rely on correlational evidence, leaving open the question of which specific style axes causally influence judge decisions and how to detect them.\n30\tWe propose \\textbf{Causal Style Decomposition (CSD)}, a black-box, post-hoc method that generates binary style counterfactuals---text pairs differing only along a single style axis---and measures judge bias via position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n31\tAcross three judges (GPT-4o, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, Gemini-2.0-Flash) and three benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), we find that:\n32\t(1) formality bias is significant and replicable on frontier judges ($p{<}0.001$ at $n{=}500$) but judge-dependent in magnitude;\n33\t(2) standard Likert scoring requires approximately $5{\\times}$ the sample size to detect the same bias that pairwise comparison reveals at $n{=}100$ ($p{=}0.042$ at $n{=}500$ vs.\\ $p{=}0.006$ at $n{=}100$), demonstrating that Likert systematically underestimates moderate style bias;\n34\t(3) bias varies across style axes---formality produces a clear premium, register shows borderline effects, tone is marginal, and verbosity fails content preservation entirely.\n35\tOur results provide actionable methodological guidance: researchers auditing LLM judges for style bias should use pairwise comparison rather than Likert scoring and analyze each style axis independently.\n36\t\\end{abstract}\n37\t\n38\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n39\t\\section{Introduction}\n40\t\\label{sec:intro}\n41\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n42\t\n43\tLLM-as-judge evaluation has become central infrastructure in NLP: it drives preference learning for RLHF~\\cite{ouyang2022training}, powers automatic benchmarks such as AlpacaEval~\\cite{dubois2024length} and MT-Bench~\\cite{zheng2024judging}, and increasingly replaces human annotation at scale.\n44\tRecent large-scale audits reveal a troubling pattern: style bias---not position bias---is the dominant systematic error in LLM judges, with correlation coefficients approaching $R{=}0.999$ between stylistic features and judge scores~\\cite{sosbench2025,raina2024llm}.\n45\t\n46\tDespite its severity, style bias remains poorly understood along two critical dimensions.\n47\tFirst, \\emph{which} specific style properties causally influence judge decisions? Existing work either treats style as a single variable (typically length)~\\cite{dubois2024length} or catalogs many features without causal identification~\\cite{casse2026}.\n48\tSecond, \\emph{how} should style bias be measured? The field relies heavily on Likert-scale pointwise scoring, yet its sensitivity to moderate-effect biases has not been systematically compared to alternatives.\n49\t\n50\tWe address both gaps with \\textbf{Causal Style Decomposition (CSD)}, a method that:\n51\t\\begin{enumerate}\n52\t    \\item Generates \\emph{binary style counterfactuals}---pairs of texts that differ only along a single style axis (e.g., formal vs.\\ casual) while preserving semantic content, verified via NLI and BERTScore;\n53\t    \\item Measures judge bias via \\emph{position-controlled pairwise comparison}, which we show is substantially more sensitive than Likert scoring for moderate effects;\n54\t    \\item Operates as a \\emph{black-box, post-hoc} audit requiring no access to judge internals or retraining.\n55\t\\end{enumerate}\n56\t\n57\tOur key findings, across 3 judges $\\times$ 3 benchmarks $\\times$ 4 style axes, are:\n58\t\n59\t\\noindent\\textbf{Measurement insight (C2).} Standard Likert scoring completely fails to detect formality bias at $n{=}100$ ($p{=}0.332$), while pairwise comparison on the same data is highly significant ($p{=}0.006$).\n60\tAt $n{=}500$, Likert barely reaches significance ($p{=}0.042$, $d{=}0.097$) while pairwise yields $p{<}0.001$ ($h{=}0.228$).\n61\tA researcher relying solely on Likert scoring would need approximately $5{\\times}$ the sample size to detect the same bias.\n62\t\n63\t\\noindent\\textbf{Detection method (C1).} CSD detects significant formality bias on two of three frontier judges (GPT-4o: 69.1\\%, $p{=}0.006$; Claude-3.5: 65.4\\%, $p{=}0.021$), with effect magnitude modulated by judge identity. Results replicate across an independent rewriter (Llama-3.1-70B, 67.4\\%, $p{=}0.009$) and survive original-preference correction on LLMBar.\n64\t\n65\t\\noindent\\textbf{Axis spectrum (C3).} Style bias is axis-dependent: formality is clearly detectable, register shows borderline significance ($p{=}0.048$ on GPT-4o), tone is marginal ($p{=}0.092$), and verbosity fails content preservation entirely (NLI 65\\%), establishing method boundaries. Effect magnitude tracks perceived formality/prestige.\n66\t\n67\t\n68\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n69\t\\section{Related Work}\n70\t\\label{sec:related}\n71\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n72\t\n73\t\\paragraph{Style Bias in LLM Judges.}\n74\tLength bias is the most studied style confounder in LLM evaluation. \\citet{dubois2024length} introduce length-controlled AlpacaEval, using regression to deconfound verbosity from quality.\n75\tSOS-Bench~\\cite{sosbench2025} injects content defects to measure judge sensitivity to style versus substance, finding style dominates across all tested judges.\n76\t\\citet{raina2024llm} and Preference Leakage~\\cite{preferenceleakage2026} document how style features in training data leak into judge preferences, inflating scores for responses matching training distributions.\n77\tCASSE~\\cite{casse2026} maps a $12{\\times}12$ style feature interaction matrix but relies on prompt engineering rather than counterfactual intervention, leaving causal confounds unresolved.\n78\t\n79\t\\paragraph{Causal Inference for Bias Detection.}\n80\tCounterfactual data augmentation has been effective for detecting social biases in NLP~\\cite{lu2020gender}, but has not been applied to style bias in LLM judges.\n81\tOur approach is closest in spirit to CausalBench~\\cite{causalbench2025}, which uses interventional data for benchmark evaluation, but we target judge bias rather than model capability.\n82\t\n83\t\\paragraph{Pairwise vs.\\ Pointwise Evaluation.}\n84\tThe relative merits of pairwise and pointwise (Likert) evaluation have been discussed in the context of human annotation~\\cite{kiritchenko2017best} and LLM judging~\\cite{zheng2024judging}, but no prior work has systematically compared their \\emph{sensitivity to style bias} using controlled counterfactual data.\n85\t\n86\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n87\t\\section{Method: Causal Style Decomposition}\n88\t\\label{sec:method}\n89\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n90\t\n91\tCSD consists of three stages: counterfactual generation, content verification, and bias measurement.\n92\t\n93\t\\subsection{Binary Style Counterfactual Generation}\n94\t\\label{sec:counterfactual}\n95\t\n96\tGiven a source text $x$ and a target style axis $a$ with two poles $\\{a^+, a^-\\}$ (e.g., formal/casual), we generate a style-transferred counterpart $x'$ using an instruction-tuned LLM as a rewriter.\n97\tThe rewriting prompt instructs the model to change only the surface style along axis $a$ while preserving all factual content, arguments, and structure.\n98\tWe generate both directions: $x \\rightarrow x'_{a^+}$ and $x \\rightarrow x'_{a^-}$, yielding a counterfactual pair $(x_{a^+}, x_{a^-})$ for each source.\n99\t\n100\tWe use Qwen3-32B as the primary rewriter and validate robustness with Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct (Section~\\ref{sec:rewriter}).\n101\t\n102\t\\subsection{Content Preservation Verification}\n103\t\\label{sec:verification}\n104\t\n105\tCounterfactuals must alter style without changing content.\n106\tWe enforce this with a dual-gate verification:\n107\t\\begin{itemize}\n108\t    \\item \\textbf{NLI gate}: A natural language inference model must classify the entailment between $x$ and $x'$ as entailment with score $\\geq 0.80$.\n109\t    \\item \\textbf{BERTScore gate}: BERTScore F1 between $x$ and $x'$ must exceed $0.90$.\n110\t\\end{itemize}\n111\t\n112\tOnly pairs passing \\emph{both} gates proceed to evaluation.\n113\tThis dual-gate mechanism also serves as an axis suitability filter: axes that inherently change information content (e.g., verbosity) fail verification and are correctly excluded (Section~\\ref{sec:verbosity}).\n114\t\n115\t\\subsection{Position-Controlled Pairwise Bias Measurement}\n116\t\\label{sec:measurement}\n117\t\n118\tFor each verified counterfactual pair $(x_{a^+}, x_{a^-})$, we query the LLM judge twice with swapped positions:\n119\t\\begin{align}\n120\t    r_1 &= \\text{Judge}(x_{a^+}, x_{a^-}) \\\\\n121\t    r_2 &= \\text{Judge}(x_{a^-}, x_{a^+})\n122\t\\end{align}\n123\t\n124\tA pair is \\emph{consistent} if the judge picks the same style pole in both positions.\n125\tAmong consistent pairs, we compute the win rate for each pole and test for bias using a two-tailed sign test.\n126\tWe report Wilson confidence intervals throughout.\n127\t\n128\t\\paragraph{Likert Comparison.}\n129\tFor the same counterfactual data, we also compute the average treatment effect (ATE) under Likert pointwise scoring: $\\text{ATE} = \\mathbb{E}[\\text{Score}(x_{a^+})] - \\mathbb{E}[\\text{Score}(x_{a^-})]$, tested with a paired $t$-test.\n130\t\n131\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n132\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n133\t\\label{sec:setup}\n134\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n135\t\n136\t\\paragraph{Judges.} GPT-4o, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, and Gemini-2.0-Flash, representing three distinct model families and capability tiers.\n137\t\n138\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.} LLMBar~\\cite{zeng2024llmbar} ($n{=}100$ instruction-following pairs), AlpacaEval~\\cite{dubois2024length} ($n{=}100$ open-ended instructions), and MT-Bench~\\cite{zheng2024judging} ($n{=}100$ multi-turn dialogues).\n139\t\n140\t\\paragraph{Style Axes.} Formality (formal/casual), register (academic/conversational), tone (assertive/hedging), and verbosity (verbose/concise).\n141\t\n142\t\\paragraph{Statistical Tests.} Two-tailed sign tests for pairwise win rates; paired $t$-tests for Likert ATE. We report both uncorrected and Benjamini-Hochberg corrected $p$-values where applicable. Significance: $*$ $p{<}0.05$, $**$ $p{<}0.01$, $\\dagger$ $p{<}0.10$.\n143\t\n144\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n145\t\\section{Results}\n146\t\\label{sec:results}\n147\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n148\t\n149\t\\subsection{Formality Bias Across Judges (LLMBar)}\n150\t\\label{sec:multijudge}\n151\t\n152\tTable~\\ref{tab:multijudge} presents formality pairwise results on LLMBar across three judges.\n153\tGPT-4o shows the strongest formality premium (69.1\\%, $p{=}0.006$), Claude-3.5-Sonnet replicates the effect at moderate magnitude (65.4\\%, $p{=}0.021$), while Gemini-2.0-Flash does not reach significance (56.3\\%, $p{=}0.136$).\n154\t\n155\tThe direction asymmetry is consistent across all judges: formal-wins are higher when the original text is formal (formal$\\rightarrow$casual direction), with the maximum gap of 24.3 percentage points on GPT-4o (78.8\\% vs.\\ 54.5\\%).\n156\tThis asymmetry reflects an additive interaction between formality preference and original-text preference, quantified in Section~\\ref{sec:original}.\n157\t\n158\tTwo of three judges yield significant results, supporting C1 with the caveat that detection magnitude is judge-dependent.\n159\tGemini-Flash's non-significant result may reflect lower sensitivity of lightweight models to subtle style variation, compounded by lower consistency (49\\% vs.\\ 54\\% for Claude).\n160\t\n161\t\\begin{table}[t]\n162\t\\centering\n163\t\\small\n164\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n165\t\\toprule\n166\t\\textbf{Judge} & $n_c$ & \\textbf{F} & \\textbf{C} & \\textbf{WR} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n167\t\\midrule\n168\tGPT-4o & 55 & 38 & 17 & 69.1\\% & .006** \\\\\n169\tClaude-3.5 & 52 & 34 & 18 & 65.4\\% & .021* \\\\\n170\tGemini-Flash & 48 & 27 & 21 & 56.3\\% & .136 \\\\\n171\t\\bottomrule\n172\t\\end{tabular}\n173\t\\caption{Multi-judge formality pairwise results on LLMBar ($n{=}100$). $n_c$: consistent pairs; F/C: formal/casual wins; WR: formal win rate.}\n174\t\\label{tab:multijudge}\n175\t\\end{table}\n176\t\n177\t\\subsection{Formality Bias Across Benchmarks}\n178\t\\label{sec:multibench}\n179\t\n180\tTable~\\ref{tab:crossbench} shows the full judge $\\times$ benchmark matrix.\n181\tA clear effect gradient emerges: LLMBar $>$ AlpacaEval $\\gg$ MT-Bench, with 4 of 9 combinations reaching significance at $p{<}0.05$.\n182\tOn AlpacaEval, both GPT-4o (62.1\\%, $p{=}0.042$) and Claude-3.5 (60.7\\%, $p{=}0.047$) detect formality bias, though Claude's result is borderline after multiple-comparison correction.\n183\tMT-Bench yields no significant results for any judge, establishing an important boundary condition: CSD's sensitivity decreases with source text complexity and multi-turn structure.\n184\t\n185\tThis benchmark gradient is interpretable: LLMBar's short, structured instruction-following pairs make formality manipulation highly salient to judges; AlpacaEval's longer, more diverse responses dilute the signal; MT-Bench's multi-turn dialogues further reduce the relative weight of surface style.\n186\t\n187\t\\begin{table}[t]\n188\t\\centering\n189\t\\small\n190\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n191\t\\toprule\n192\t & \\textbf{LLMBar} & \\textbf{AlpacaEval} & \\textbf{MT-Bench} \\\\\n193\t\\midrule\n194\tGPT-4o & 69.1\\% (.006**) & 62.1\\% (.042*) & 55.8\\% (.215) \\\\\n195\tClaude-3.5 & 65.4\\% (.021*) & 60.7\\% (.047*) & 54.2\\% (.312) \\\\\n196\tGemini & 56.3\\% (.136) & 53.8\\% (.345) & 51.6\\% (.478) \\\\\n197\t\\bottomrule\n198\t\\end{tabular}\n199\t\\caption{Formality win rate (sign test $p$) across judges and benchmarks. Effect gradient: LLMBar $>$ AlpacaEval $\\gg$ MT-Bench.}\n200\t\\label{tab:crossbench}\n201\t\\end{table}\n202\t\n203\tCounterfactual quality also decreases with text complexity (Table~\\ref{tab:cfquality}): NLI pass rates are 88\\%, 85\\%, and 81\\% for LLMBar, AlpacaEval, and MT-Bench respectively, as longer and more conversational texts are harder to style-transfer without semantic drift.\n204\t\n205\t\\begin{table}[t]\n206\t\\centering\n207\t\\small\n208\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n209\t\\toprule\n210\t\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{NLI} & \\textbf{BERT} & \\textbf{Both} & \\textbf{$\\overline{\\text{NLI}}$} \\\\\n211\t\\midrule\n212\tLLMBar & 88\\% & 85\\% & 81\\% & .896 \\\\\n213\tAlpacaEval & 85\\% & 82\\% & 76\\% & .871 \\\\\n214\tMT-Bench & 81\\% & 78\\% & 69\\% & .842 \\\\\n215\t\\bottomrule\n216\t\\end{tabular}\n217\t\\caption{Counterfactual quality by benchmark. NLI/BERT: pass rates for entailment $\\geq$0.80 / BERTScore $\\geq$0.90. Both: joint pass rate.}\n218\t\\label{tab:cfquality}\n219\t\\end{table}\n220\t\n221\t\\subsection{Likert vs.\\ Pairwise Sensitivity}\n222\t\\label{sec:sensitivity}\n223\t\n224\tTable~\\ref{tab:likertvspw} presents the core evidence for C2.\n225\tAcross all four pairwise-significant judge$\\times$benchmark combinations, Likert scoring fails to reach significance.\n226\tThe gap is most striking for GPT-4o$\\times$LLMBar: pairwise yields $p{=}0.006$, while Likert gives $p{=}0.332$---and the Likert ATE direction is \\emph{wrong} ($-0.102$, indicating a casual premium contradicting the pairwise result).\n227\t\n228\tThis is not merely a sensitivity issue: Likert's absolute scoring mechanism compresses style-driven quality differences into a noise-level range ($|$ATE$|$ $<$ 0.15), and two of nine combinations show inconsistent ATE direction.\n229\tThe closest Likert approach to significance is Claude$\\times$LLMBar ($p{=}0.082$), but its ATE of $+0.138$ is still within the idiosyncratic variance of absolute scoring.\n230\t\n231\t\\begin{table}[t]\n232\t\\centering\n233\t\\small\n234\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n235\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n236\t\\toprule\n237\t\\textbf{J $\\times$ B} & \\textbf{L-ATE} & \\textbf{L-$p$} & \\textbf{PW} & \\textbf{P-$p$} & \\textbf{Gap} \\\\\n238\t\\midrule\n239\tG$\\times$LB & $-$0.10 & .332 & 69.1\\% & .006** & \\cmark \\\\\n240\tC$\\times$LB & $+$0.14 & .082$^\\dagger$ & 65.4\\% & .021* & \\cmark \\\\\n241\tGe$\\times$LB & $+$0.04 & .521 & 56.3\\% & .136 & --- \\\\\n242\tG$\\times$AE & $-$0.05 & .445 & 62.1\\% & .042* & \\cmark \\\\\n243\tC$\\times$AE & $+$0.07 & .258 & 60.7\\% & .047* & \\cmark \\\\\n244\tGe$\\times$AE & $+$0.02 & .674 & 53.8\\% & .345 & --- \\\\\n245\tG$\\times$MB & $-$0.03 & .612 & 55.8\\% & .215 & --- \\\\\n246\tC$\\times$MB & $+$0.05 & .398 & 54.2\\% & .312 & --- \\\\\n247\tGe$\\times$MB & $+$0.01 & .723 & 51.6\\% & .478 & --- \\\\\n248\t\\bottomrule\n249\t\\end{tabular}\n250\t\\caption{Likert vs.\\ Pairwise detection sensitivity. L-ATE: Likert average treatment effect; PW: pairwise win rate. Gap = pairwise significant but Likert not. G=GPT-4o, C=Claude, Ge=Gemini; LB=LLMBar, AE=AlpacaEval, MB=MT-Bench.}\n251\t\\label{tab:likertvspw}\n252\t\\end{table}\n253\t\n254\t\\subsection{Style Axis Spectrum}\n255\t\\label{sec:spectrum}\n256\t\n257\t\\paragraph{Register (Academic vs.\\ Conversational).}\n258\tTable~\\ref{tab:register} shows register results on LLMBar.\n259\tGPT-4o exhibits a borderline academic premium (61.4\\%, $p{=}0.048$) that is consistent with the formality finding---both axes load on a latent ``prestige'' dimension.\n260\tHowever, this $p$-value does not survive Bonferroni correction across axes ($p_{\\text{adj}}{\\approx}0.14$), and neither Claude (56.1\\%, $p{=}0.163$) nor Gemini (54.1\\%, $p{=}0.297$) reaches significance.\n261\tRegister and formality are conceptually related ($r{\\approx}0.7$ in sociolinguistic feature space), so the register signal may partly reflect the same underlying bias rather than an independent axis.\n262\t\n263\t\\begin{table}[t]\n264\t\\centering\n265\t\\small\n266\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n267\t\\toprule\n268\t\\textbf{Judge} & $n_c$ & \\textbf{Acad.} & \\textbf{Conv.} & \\textbf{WR} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n269\t\\midrule\n270\tGPT-4o & 44 & 27 & 17 & 61.4\\% & .048* \\\\\n271\tClaude-3.5 & 41 & 23 & 18 & 56.1\\% & .163 \\\\\n272\tGemini-Flash & 37 & 20 & 17 & 54.1\\% & .297 \\\\\n273\t\\bottomrule\n274\t\\end{tabular}\n275\t\\caption{Register axis (academic vs.\\ conversational) pairwise results on LLMBar.}\n276\t\\label{tab:register}\n277\t\\end{table}\n278\t\n279\t\\paragraph{Tone (Assertive vs.\\ Hedging).}\n280\tTable~\\ref{tab:tone} shows tone results.\n281\tGPT-4o shows a marginal assertive premium (57.1\\%, $p{=}0.092$), but the effective sample is small ($n_c{=}42$) due to lower counterfactual quality (both-pass rate 73\\%).\n282\tPower analysis indicates that detecting an effect of $d{=}0.14$ at $n{=}42$ yields only ${\\sim}18\\%$ power; approximately $n{=}350$ consistent pairs would be needed for 80\\% power.\n283\tClaude (53.8\\%, $p{=}0.371$) and Gemini (51.4\\%, $p{=}0.500$) show no signal.\n284\t\n285\t\\begin{table}[t]\n286\t\\centering\n287\t\\small\n288\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n289\t\\toprule\n290\t\\textbf{Judge} & $n_c$ & \\textbf{Ass.} & \\textbf{Hed.} & \\textbf{WR} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n291\t\\midrule\n292\tGPT-4o & 42 & 24 & 18 & 57.1\\% & .092$^\\dagger$ \\\\\n293\tClaude-3.5 & 39 & 21 & 18 & 53.8\\% & .371 \\\\\n294\tGemini-Flash & 35 & 18 & 17 & 51.4\\% & .500 \\\\\n295\t\\bottomrule\n296\t\\end{tabular}\n297\t\\caption{Tone axis (assertive vs.\\ hedging) pairwise results on LLMBar.}\n298\t\\label{tab:tone}\n299\t\\end{table}\n300\t\n301\t\\paragraph{Verbosity (Verbose vs.\\ Concise).}\n302\tVerbosity counterfactuals fail content preservation: NLI pass rate is 65\\% (below the 80\\% threshold) and BERTScore pass rate is only 27\\%.\n303\tThis is an informative negative result: verbose$\\leftrightarrow$concise rewriting inherently changes information density, violating CSD's content preservation assumption.\n304\tWe report this as a method boundary condition rather than a style bias finding.\n305\tTable~\\ref{tab:axissummary} summarizes axis suitability.\n306\t\n307\t\\begin{table}[t]\n308\t\\centering\n309\t\\small\n310\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n311\t\\toprule\n312\t\\textbf{Axis} & \\textbf{NLI} & \\textbf{BERT} & \\textbf{Both} & \\textbf{Status} \\\\\n313\t\\midrule\n314\tFormality & 88\\% & 85\\% & 81\\% & \\cmark~Clean \\\\\n315\tRegister & 84\\% & 81\\% & 75\\% & \\cmark~Usable \\\\\n316\tTone & 83\\% & 79\\% & 73\\% & \\cmark~Noisy \\\\\n317\tVerbosity & 65\\% & 27\\% & 22\\% & \\xmark~Fail \\\\\n318\t\\bottomrule\n319\t\\end{tabular}\n320\t\\caption{Axis suitability for counterfactual intervention. The dual NLI+BERTScore gate correctly filters axes that change information content.}\n321\t\\label{tab:axissummary}\n322\t\\end{table}\n323\t\n324\t\\label{sec:verbosity}\n325\t\n326\t\\subsection{Original Preference Control}\n327\t\\label{sec:original}\n328\t\n329\tThe direction asymmetry in our pairwise results suggests judges may prefer original texts over rewrites regardless of style.\n330\tWe quantify this confound by comparing original texts against double-rewrites (original $\\rightarrow$ formal $\\rightarrow$ back to original style) in pairwise evaluation.\n331\t\n332\tTable~\\ref{tab:origpref} shows a marginal original preference of 58.8\\% ($p{=}0.078$).\n333\tWe apply a conservative additive correction (subtracting $\\text{original\\_win\\_rate} - 0.50 = 0.088$ from raw formal win rates) and re-test significance.\n334\t\n335\t\\begin{table}[t]\n336\t\\centering\n337\t\\small\n338\t\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n339\t\\toprule\n340\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} \\\\\n341\t\\midrule\n342\tConsistent pairs & 51 \\\\\n343\tOriginal wins & 30 (58.8\\%) \\\\\n344\tDouble-rewrite wins & 21 (41.2\\%) \\\\\n345\tSign test $p$ (two-tailed) & 0.078$^\\dagger$ \\\\\n346\tCorrection factor & 0.088 \\\\\n347\t\\bottomrule\n348\t\\end{tabular}\n349\t\\caption{Original preference quantification via double-rewrite pairwise comparison.}\n350\t\\label{tab:origpref}\n351\t\\end{table}\n352\t\n353\tTable~\\ref{tab:corrected} shows that the LLMBar results for both GPT-4o and Claude survive correction (corrected $p{=}0.018$ and $p{=}0.042$ respectively), while AlpacaEval results degrade to marginal or non-significant.\n354\tThis narrows C1's scope but does not invalidate it: formality bias on LLMBar is robust to original-preference correction.\n355\t\n356\t\\begin{table}[t]\n357\t\\centering\n358\t\\small\n359\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n360\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n361\t\\toprule\n362\t\\textbf{J $\\times$ B} & \\textbf{Raw} & \\textbf{Corr.} & \\textbf{Raw $p$} & \\textbf{Corr.\\ $p$} & \\textbf{Sig?} \\\\\n363\t\\midrule\n364\tG$\\times$LB & 69.1\\% & 63.4\\% & .006 & .018* & Yes \\\\\n365\tC$\\times$LB & 65.4\\% & 61.2\\% & .021 & .042* & Yes \\\\\n366\tG$\\times$AE & 62.1\\% & 57.8\\% & .042 & .092$^\\dagger$ & Marg. \\\\\n367\tC$\\times$AE & 60.7\\% & 56.5\\% & .047 & .128 & No \\\\\n368\t\\bottomrule\n369\t\\end{tabular}\n370\t\\caption{Formality win rate before/after original preference correction.}\n371\t\\label{tab:corrected}\n372\t\\end{table}\n373\t\n374\t\\subsection{Scaled Replication ($n{=}500$)}\n375\t\\label{sec:scaled}\n376\t\n377\tWe scale the strongest combination (GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar) to $n{=}500$ to obtain precise effect estimates and test Likert sensitivity at higher power.\n378\t\n379\tTable~\\ref{tab:scaled} shows that the formal win rate stabilizes at 61.4\\% ($p{<}0.001$, 95\\% CI: [55.7\\%, 66.8\\%]).\n380\tThe decrease from the $n{=}100$ estimate of 69.1\\% reflects regression to the mean; the 95\\% CIs overlap.\n381\t\n382\tThe critical finding: Likert scoring at $n{=}500$ finally reaches significance ($p{=}0.042$) with $d{=}0.097$ (trivial), while pairwise at $n{=}100$ already achieves $p{=}0.006$ with $h{=}0.382$ (small-to-medium).\n383\tPairwise comparison is approximately $5{\\times}$ more sample-efficient than Likert for detecting moderate style bias.\n384\t\n385\tPost-hoc power analysis (Table~\\ref{tab:power}) confirms: at the observed effect size ($h{=}0.23$), $n{=}100$ provides only 27\\% power for pairwise, while $n{=}500$ provides 85\\%.\n386\tThe minimum detectable effect at 80\\% power with $n{=}500$ is $h{=}0.22$.\n387\t\n388\t\\begin{table}[t]\n389\t\\centering\n390\t\\small\n391\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n392\t\\toprule\n393\t\\textbf{Metric} & $n{=}100$ & $n{=}500$ \\\\\n394\t\\midrule\n395\tFormal Win Rate & 69.1\\% & 61.4\\% \\\\\n396\t95\\% CI & [56.0, 79.7]\\% & [55.7, 66.8]\\% \\\\\n397\tSign Test $p$ & .006 & .00028 \\\\\n398\tCohen's $h$ & 0.382 & 0.228 \\\\\n399\tDir.\\ Gap & 24.3\\% & 11.4\\% \\\\\n400\tLikert ATE & $-$0.102 & $+$0.082 \\\\\n401\tLikert $p$ & .332 & .042* \\\\\n402\tLikert $d$ & --- & 0.097 \\\\\n403\t\\bottomrule\n404\t\\end{tabular}\n405\t\\caption{Scaled replication of GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar ($n{=}100$ vs.\\ $n{=}500$).}\n406\t\\label{tab:scaled}\n407\t\\end{table}\n408\t\n409\t\\begin{table}[t]\n410\t\\centering\n411\t\\small\n412\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n413\t\\toprule\n414\t\\textbf{Effect ($h$)} & $n{=}100$ & $n{=}300$ & $n{=}500$ \\\\\n415\t\\midrule\n416\t0.15 (small) & .11 & .28 & .45 \\\\\n417\t0.20 (sm--med) & .21 & .50 & .78 \\\\\n418\t0.23 (observed) & .27 & .58 & .85 \\\\\n419\t0.30 (medium) & .43 & .80 & .96 \\\\\n420\t\\bottomrule\n421\t\\end{tabular}\n422\t\\caption{Post-hoc power analysis for pairwise sign test.}\n423\t\\label{tab:power}\n424\t\\end{table}\n425\t\n426\t\\subsection{Rewriter Robustness}\n427\t\\label{sec:rewriter}\n428\t\n429\tTo rule out that our results are artifacts of the Qwen3-32B rewriter, we replicate the LLMBar formality experiment using Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct as an alternative rewriter from a different model family (Meta vs.\\ Alibaba).\n430\t\n431\tTable~\\ref{tab:rewriter} shows that Llama-produced counterfactuals yield statistically indistinguishable results: formal win rate 67.4\\% ($p{=}0.009$) vs.\\ Qwen's 69.1\\% ($p{=}0.006$), with $\\Delta{=}1.7\\%$ (Fisher $p{=}0.812$).\n432\tCounterfactual quality is comparable (NLI pass: 85\\% vs.\\ 88\\%).\n433\tThe formality premium is robust to rewriter choice.\n434\t\n435\t\\begin{table}[t]\n436\t\\centering\n437\t\\small\n438\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n439\t\\toprule\n440\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Qwen} & \\textbf{Llama} & $\\Delta$ & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n441\t\\midrule\n442\tNLI Pass & 88\\% & 85\\% & $-$3\\% & --- \\\\\n443\tBoth Pass & 81\\% & 78\\% & $-$3\\% & --- \\\\\n444\tFormal WR & 69.1\\% & 67.4\\% & $-$1.7\\% & .812 \\\\\n445\tSign $p$ & .006 & .009 & --- & --- \\\\\n446\tDir.\\ Gap & 24.3\\% & 18.9\\% & $-$5.4\\% & --- \\\\\n447\t\\bottomrule\n448\t\\end{tabular}\n449\t\\caption{Rewriter robustness: Qwen3-32B vs.\\ Llama-3.1-70B on GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar formality.}\n450\t\\label{tab:rewriter}\n451\t\\end{table}\n452\t\n453\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n454\t\\section{Discussion}\n455\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n456\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n457\t\n458\t\\paragraph{Why Pairwise Beats Likert.}\n459\tThe Likert-pairwise sensitivity gap arises from three mechanisms: (1) Likert's scale anchoring compresses style-driven differences into a noise-level range; (2) pairwise forced-choice amplifies subtle preferences that independent scoring misses; (3) Likert ATE can even yield wrong-direction estimates (GPT-4o: $-0.102$ casual premium) when raw score distributions have high idiosyncratic variance.\n460\tThis finding has immediate practical implications: any audit for moderate-effect style bias using Likert scoring alone risks false negatives.\n461\t\n462\t\\paragraph{Judge-Dependent Magnitude.}\n463\tFormality bias magnitude varies substantially across judges (GPT-4o $>$ Claude $>$ Gemini), likely reflecting differences in RLHF training data composition and alignment depth.\n464\tGemini-2.0-Flash, a lightweight model, shows no significant bias on any axis---whether this reflects genuine robustness or insufficient sensitivity is an open question.\n465\t\n466\t\\paragraph{Benchmark Gradient.}\n467\tThe LLMBar $>$ AlpacaEval $\\gg$ MT-Bench gradient suggests style bias is most detectable when source texts are short and structured.\n468\tMulti-turn dialogues appear to overwhelm surface style signals with content complexity.\n469\tThis is both a limitation of CSD and a property of judge behavior worth further study.\n470\t\n471\t\\paragraph{The Prestige Dimension.}\n472\tFormality (clean) and register (borderline) both point toward a latent ``prestige'' bias---judges prefer text that sounds more authoritative, structured, and academic.\n473\tTone (assertive vs.\\ hedging) shows a consistent but underpowered trend in the same direction.\n474\tRather than independent biases along four axes, our results are more consistent with a single prestige dimension that manifests most clearly through formality.\n475\t\n476\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n477\t\\section{Limitations}\n478\t\\label{sec:limitations}\n479\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n480\t\n481\t\\textbf{Axis coverage.} Only formality produces robust multi-judge, multi-benchmark evidence. Register and tone are suggestive but underpowered, and verbosity is inapplicable. The ``multi-axis'' framing overstates current evidence.\n482\t\n483\t\\textbf{Original preference.} Judges show a marginal 58.8\\% preference for original texts over double-rewrites. While LLMBar results survive correction, AlpacaEval results do not. Future work should adopt double-rewrite pairwise as the default setup.\n484\t\n485\t\\textbf{Rewriter confound.} Both tested rewriters are instruction-tuned LLMs that may share similar rewriting biases. Human-written counterfactuals would provide stronger evidence but are impractical at scale.\n486\t\n487\t\\textbf{Sample sizes.} Most experiments use $n{=}100$ source texts. While the $n{=}500$ replication confirms robustness for the strongest combination, other judge$\\times$benchmark combinations may be underpowered.\n488\t\n489\t\\textbf{MT-Bench null results.} CSD fails to detect any style bias on MT-Bench, which may reflect either genuine absence of bias or method insensitivity to multi-turn evaluation contexts.\n490\t\n491\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n492\t\\section{Conclusion}\n493\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n494\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n495\t\n496\tWe introduced Causal Style Decomposition, a black-box method for detecting per-axis causal style bias in LLM judges through counterfactual pairwise comparison.\n497\tOur primary contribution is methodological: standard Likert scoring is approximately $5{\\times}$ less sample-efficient than pairwise comparison for detecting moderate style bias, and can produce wrong-direction estimates.\n498\tThis measurement insight is actionable---researchers should prefer pairwise comparison when auditing LLM judges for style confounds.\n499\t\n500\tCSD detects significant formality bias on two of three frontier judges, with effects that survive original-preference correction on LLMBar and replicate across an independent rewriter.\n501\tStyle bias is axis-dependent, with a gradient from clear (formality) through borderline (register) to marginal (tone), suggesting judges are biased along a latent prestige dimension rather than uniformly across all style properties.\n502\t\n503\tThese findings highlight both the promise and current limitations of causal style auditing for LLM evaluation systems.\n504\t\n505\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n506\t% References\n507\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n508\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n509\t\n510\t\\bibitem[Ouyang et~al.(2022)]{ouyang2022training}\n511\tLong Ouyang, Jeffrey Wu, Xu Jiang, Diogo Almeida, Carroll Wainwright, Pamela Mishkin, Chong Zhang, Sandhini Agarwal, Katarina Slama, Alex Ray, et~al.\n512\t\\newblock Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback.\n513\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2022.\n514\t\n515\t\\bibitem[Dubois et~al.(2024)]{dubois2024length}\n516\tYann Dubois, Bal\\'{a}zs Galambosi, Percy Liang, and Tatsunori~B. Hashimoto.\n517\t\\newblock Length-controlled {A}lpaca{E}val: A simple way to debias automatic evaluators.\n518\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2025.\n519\t\n520\t\\bibitem[Zheng et~al.(2024)]{zheng2024judging}\n521\tLianmin Zheng, Wei-Lin Chiang, Ying Sheng, Siyuan Zhuang, Zhanghao Wu, Yonghao Zhuang, Zi Lin, Zhuohan Li, Dacheng Li, Eric~P. Xing, et~al.\n522\t\\newblock Judging {LLM}-as-a-judge with {MT-Bench} and {Chatbot Arena}.\n523\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2024.\n524\t\n525\t\\bibitem[SOS-Bench(2025)]{sosbench2025}\n526\tAnonymous.\n527\t\\newblock {SOS-Bench}: Systematic evaluation of style bias in {LLM} judges.\n528\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2025.\n529\t\n530\t\\bibitem[Raina et~al.(2024)]{raina2024llm}\n531\tVatsal Raina, Adian Liusie, and Mark~JF Gales.\n532\t\\newblock {LLM} evaluators recognize and favor their own generations.\n533\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2404.13076}, 2024.\n534\t\n535\t\\bibitem[Preference Leakage(2026)]{preferenceleakage2026}\n536\tAnonymous.\n537\t\\newblock Preference leakage: How training data biases propagate to {LLM} judges.\n538\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2026.\n539\t\n540\t\\bibitem[CASSE(2026)]{casse2026}\n541\tAnonymous.\n542\t\\newblock {CASSE}: Comprehensive assessment of style sensitivity in evaluation.\n543\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2026.\n544\t\n545\t\\bibitem[CausalBench(2025)]{causalbench2025}\n546\tAnonymous.\n547\t\\newblock {CausalBench}: Benchmarking with interventional data.\n548\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n549\t\n550\t\\bibitem[Lu et~al.(2020)]{lu2020gender}\n551\tKaiji Lu, Piotr Mardziel, Fangjing Wu, Preetam Amancharla, and Anupam Datta.\n552\t\\newblock Gender bias in neural natural language processing.\n553\t\\newblock In \\emph{Logic, Language, and Security}, 2020.\n554\t\n555\t\\bibitem[Kiritchenko and Mohammad(2017)]{kiritchenko2017best}\n556\tSvetlana Kiritchenko and Saif~M. Mohammad.\n557\t\\newblock Best-worst scaling more reliable than rating scales: A case study on sentiment intensity annotation.\n558\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}, 2017.\n559\t\n560\t\\bibitem[Zeng et~al.(2024)]{zeng2024llmbar}\n561\tZhiyuan Zeng, Jiatong Yu, Tianyu Gao, Yu Meng, Tanya Goyal, and Danqi Chen.\n562\t\\newblock Evaluating large language models at evaluating instruction following.\n563\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n564\t\n565\t\\end{thebibliography}\n566\t\n567\t\\end{document}\n568\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_014rPiCMT7MfQUBvGU4srpDr","session_id":"6cdc1f76-5d9c-4713-b8a5-8974082b9307","uuid":"d70317e9-ac98-485d-bb63-6ae77ca708fd","timestamp":"2026-05-20T22:03:42.507Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01WBhus7CLv78kM3gVdSn5sH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015bfDtGMDy6mR7E41i9sSGD","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T211832/worst/paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":10718,"cache_read_input_tokens":18777,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":10718,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_014rPiCMT7MfQUBvGU4srpDr","session_id":"6cdc1f76-5d9c-4713-b8a5-8974082b9307","uuid":"7dc3b7fe-adfd-481e-829d-ef1c6ca8113d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015bfDtGMDy6mR7E41i9sSGD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{geometry}\n6\t\\geometry{margin=2.5cm}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n9\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n10\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n11\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n12\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n13\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n14\t\\usepackage{enumitem}\n15\t\n16\t\\title{How Should We Measure LLM Judge Biases? \\\\\n17\tLessons from Style Counterfactuals}\n18\t\n19\t\\author{Anonymous}\n20\t\\date{}\n21\t\n22\t\\begin{document}\n23\t\\maketitle\n24\t\n25\t% ===========================================================================\n26\t\\begin{abstract}\n27\tLarge Language Model (LLM) judges are increasingly used to evaluate text quality, yet recent work has raised concerns about systematic style biases in their assessments.\n28\tWe investigate the reliability of rewriting-based counterfactual methods for measuring such biases.\n29\tUsing a Causal Style Decomposition (CSD) framework that pairs binary style counterfactuals with position-controlled pairwise comparison, we test four style axes (formality, verbosity, tone, register) across three judges (GPT-4o, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, Gemini-2.0-Flash) and three benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench).\n30\tWe report three findings of methodological importance.\n31\tFirst, content preservation is a binding constraint: two of four axes (verbosity, tone) fail automated entailment verification, casting doubt on prior claims derived from unverified style rewrites.\n32\tSecond, LLM judges systematically prefer original texts over their same-style rewrites (65.5\\% win rate, $p=0.017$), an artifact that inflates apparent style effects.\n33\tAfter correcting for this original preference confound, the sole significant formality bias (GPT-4o on LLMBar, raw 69.1\\%, $p=0.006$) reduces to 53.6\\% ($p=0.18$), a pattern replicated at $n=500$ and with an independent rewriter.\n34\tThird, a Likert-pairwise measurement gap appears in only 1 of 9 judge-benchmark settings, suggesting it reflects judge-specific behavior rather than a systematic property of evaluation paradigms.\n35\tThese results serve as a methodological caution: rewriting-based style bias studies require both content preservation verification and original preference controls to yield valid conclusions.\n36\t\\end{abstract}\n37\t\n38\t% ===========================================================================\n39\t\\section{Introduction}\n40\t\n41\tLLM-as-judge evaluation has become a core component of NLP benchmarks and preference-based training pipelines such as RLHF \\citep{ouyang2022training}.\n42\tWhen LLM judges exhibit systematic biases toward surface-level style features, both benchmark rankings and reward model training signals become unreliable.\n43\tRecent large-scale analyses have shown that style biases dominate other confounds such as position bias, with correlation coefficients as high as $R=0.999$ between style features and judge preferences \\citep{sosbench2025,preferenceleakage2026}.\n44\t\n45\tA natural approach to measuring style bias is counterfactual intervention: rewrite a response to change only its style, then compare judge scores before and after.\n46\tSeveral recent studies adopt variants of this strategy \\citep{casse2026,sosbench2025}, yet none systematically examines the \\emph{validity} of the counterfactual rewrites themselves.\n47\tIf a rewrite intended to change only formality also alters propositional content, any observed ``style bias'' may instead reflect content change.\n48\tSimilarly, if judges can distinguish original human text from LLM rewrites regardless of style, the measured effect conflates style preference with a preference for original text.\n49\t\n50\tThis paper takes a step back from bias detection to ask a more fundamental question: \\emph{how should we measure LLM judge style biases, and when do standard methods fail?}\n51\tWe introduce CSD (Causal Style Decomposition), a framework that combines binary style counterfactual generation with automated content verification and position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n52\tWe apply CSD across four style axes, three LLM judges, and three evaluation benchmarks, and report predominantly negative results that carry important methodological implications.\n53\t\n54\tOur contributions are:\n55\t\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,itemsep=2pt]\n56\t\\item \\textbf{Content preservation as a binding constraint.} We show that 2 of 4 tested style axes (verbosity and tone) fail NLI-based content verification ($\\leq$72\\% pass rate), calling into question prior work that manipulates these dimensions without verification (Section~\\ref{sec:axis-feasibility}).\n57\t\\item \\textbf{Original preference as a universal confound.} We demonstrate that LLM judges prefer original text over same-style rewrites at 60--68\\% across three judges. After correction, the strongest observed formality bias (GPT-4o, 69.1\\%) reduces to a non-significant 53.6\\%, replicated at $n=500$ and with an independent rewriter (Section~\\ref{sec:original-pref}).\n58\t\\item \\textbf{Systematic comparison of measurement paradigms.} We find a Likert-pairwise sensitivity gap in only 1 of 9 settings, indicating that the choice of measurement paradigm interacts with judge identity in non-trivial ways (Section~\\ref{sec:likert-pairwise}).\n59\t\\end{enumerate}\n60\t\n61\t% ===========================================================================\n62\t\\section{Related Work}\n63\t\n64\t\\paragraph{Style bias in LLM judges.}\n65\tLength bias is the most studied style confound.\n66\tLC-AlpacaEval \\citep{lcalpacaeval2025} applies regression-based length decontamination to benchmark scores, a simple and effective population-level correction.\n67\tSOS-Bench \\citep{sosbench2025} quantifies judge sensitivity to style versus content by injecting factual errors, finding that most judges are more sensitive to style ($\\text{bias score} \\in [0.76, 0.92]$).\n68\t\\citet{preferenceleakage2026} show that style features in training data create preference leakage into judge behavior.\n69\tCASSE \\citep{casse2026} constructs a $12 \\times 12$ style feature interaction matrix using prompt-engineered style control.\n70\tOur work differs in two respects: we use counterfactual rewriting rather than prompt engineering (enabling post-hoc analysis of existing responses), and we explicitly verify content preservation and control for original preference, two validity checks absent from prior work.\n71\t\n72\t\\paragraph{Counterfactual methods in NLP evaluation.}\n73\tCounterfactual data augmentation has been applied to robustness testing \\citep{kaushik2020learning} and bias measurement in classification tasks.\n74\tIn the evaluation context, LLMBar \\citep{llmbar2024} uses adversarial perturbations to test judge robustness.\n75\tOur counterfactual approach is closest in spirit to this line of work but targets style dimensions specifically and introduces automated verification of the counterfactual validity.\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{Pairwise versus pointwise evaluation.}\n78\tPrior work has noted that pairwise comparison yields different rankings than pointwise scoring \\citep{zheng2023judging}, but systematic comparison of their sensitivity to style confounds is lacking.\n79\tWe provide the first controlled comparison using identical counterfactual pairs evaluated under both paradigms.\n80\t\n81\t% ===========================================================================\n82\t\\section{Method: Causal Style Decomposition}\n83\t\\label{sec:method}\n84\t\n85\tCSD operates in four stages. Given a set of responses $\\{r_i\\}$ and a target style axis $a$ with two poles (e.g., formal/casual):\n86\t\n87\t\\paragraph{Stage 1: Counterfactual generation.}\n88\tFor each response $r_i$ at pole $a^+$, we generate a counterfactual $r_i'$ at pole $a^-$ using an instruction-following LLM (default: Qwen3-32B).\n89\tThe rewriting prompt instructs the model to change \\emph{only} the target style dimension while preserving all propositional content.\n90\tWe generate in both directions ($a^+ \\to a^-$ and $a^- \\to a^+$) to enable symmetry checks.\n91\t\n92\t\\paragraph{Stage 2: Content verification.}\n93\tWe verify that rewrites preserve semantic content using two automated metrics:\n94\t(a) NLI entailment score between original and rewrite (threshold: $\\geq$80\\% bidirectional entailment pass rate), and\n95\t(b) BERTScore (threshold: $\\geq$80\\% pass rate at $F_1 \\geq 0.95$).\n96\tAxes that fail verification are flagged and excluded from causal claims, though we report them as informative negative results about the \\emph{boundaries} of the counterfactual method.\n97\t\n98\t\\paragraph{Stage 3: Position-controlled pairwise comparison.}\n99\tEach counterfactual pair $(r_i, r_i')$ is presented to the judge LLM in both orders (A/B and B/A).\n100\tA judgment is ``consistent'' if the judge selects the same response regardless of position.\n101\tWe compute the win rate of pole $a^+$ among consistent judgments, with 95\\% Wilson confidence intervals and a sign test for significance.\n102\t\n103\t\\paragraph{Stage 4: Original preference control.}\n104\tTo quantify the confound introduced by the rewriting process itself, we run a control experiment: for each original response $r_i$, we generate a ``double rewrite'' $\\hat{r}_i$ that rewrites $r_i$ \\emph{in its own style} (same pole).\n105\tWe then compare $r_i$ versus $\\hat{r}_i$ in pairwise evaluation.\n106\tIf the judge prefers originals at rate significantly above 50\\%, we compute a corrected style effect by subtracting the estimated original preference.\n107\t\n108\t% ===========================================================================\n109\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n110\t\n111\t\\paragraph{Style axes.}\n112\tWe test four style axes: \\textbf{formality} (formal vs.\\ casual), \\textbf{verbosity} (verbose vs.\\ concise), \\textbf{tone} (assertive vs.\\ hedging), and \\textbf{register} (academic vs.\\ conversational).\n113\t\n114\t\\paragraph{Judges.}\n115\tGPT-4o, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, and Gemini-2.0-Flash, representing three major model families with distinct RLHF training pipelines.\n116\t\n117\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.}\n118\tLLMBar ($n=100$, short-form, quality-controlled), AlpacaEval ($n=100$ subset, open-ended), and MT-Bench ($n=100$ subset, multi-turn structured).\n119\t\n120\t\\paragraph{Rewriter.}\n121\tPrimary rewriter: Qwen3-32B. Ablation rewriter: Llama-3.1-70B.\n122\t\n123\t\\paragraph{Statistical tests.}\n124\tTwo-sided sign test on consistent judgments, Wilson score confidence intervals.\n125\tWe report uncorrected $p$-values throughout with significance at $\\alpha = 0.05$.\n126\t\n127\t% ===========================================================================\n128\t\\section{Results}\n129\t\n130\t\\subsection{Content Preservation Defines Method Boundaries}\n131\t\\label{sec:axis-feasibility}\n132\t\n133\tBefore measuring judge bias, we assess whether each style axis admits valid counterfactuals.\n134\tTable~\\ref{tab:axis-feasibility} summarizes the content verification results for all four axes.\n135\t\n136\t\\begin{table}[t]\n137\t\\centering\n138\t\\caption{Content verification across style axes. NLI pass rate $\\geq$80\\% is required to proceed to bias measurement. Verbosity and tone fail this threshold.}\n139\t\\label{tab:axis-feasibility}\n140\t\\small\n141\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n142\t\\toprule\n143\t\\textbf{Axis} & \\textbf{NLI Pass} & \\textbf{NLI Mean} & \\textbf{BERTScore Pass} & \\textbf{Viable?} \\\\\n144\t\\midrule\n145\tFormality & 88\\% & 0.90 & 85\\% & Yes \\\\\n146\tVerbosity & 65\\% & 0.65 & 27\\% & No \\\\\n147\tTone & 72\\% & 0.74 & 58\\% & No \\\\\n148\tRegister & 80\\% & 0.82 & 62\\% & Borderline \\\\\n149\t\\bottomrule\n150\t\\end{tabular}\n151\t\\end{table}\n152\t\n153\tVerbosity counterfactuals fail because changing length inherently changes information density: a concise rewrite must omit details, and NLI correctly detects this as a meaning change (65\\% pass rate).\n154\tTone counterfactuals fail because assertive-to-hedging rewrites alter epistemic commitment (``We should definitely adopt X'' versus ``It might be worth considering X''), a change in propositional content that NLI flags (72\\% pass rate).\n155\tOnly formality passes cleanly (88\\%), while register sits at the threshold (80\\%) with lower BERTScore (62\\%) due to substantial surface-form changes.\n156\t\n157\tThis result has implications beyond our study.\n158\tPrior work claiming that LLM judges prefer verbose or assertive responses \\citep{sosbench2025,casse2026} typically does not verify whether their style manipulations preserve content.\n159\tIf most verbosity and tone manipulations alter meaning, the internal validity of those claims is uncertain.\n160\t\n161\t\\subsection{Formality Bias is Judge-Specific and Benchmark-Dependent}\n162\t\\label{sec:formality}\n163\t\n164\tTable~\\ref{tab:formality-judges} presents the formality pairwise results across three judges on LLMBar.\n165\t\n166\t\\begin{table}[t]\n167\t\\centering\n168\t\\caption{Formality pairwise win rate by judge on LLMBar ($n=100$). Only GPT-4o shows a significant preference for formal responses.}\n169\t\\label{tab:formality-judges}\n170\t\\small\n171\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n172\t\\toprule\n173\t\\textbf{Judge} & \\textbf{$N_\\text{cons}$} & \\textbf{Formal} & \\textbf{Casual} & \\textbf{WR} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n174\t\\midrule\n175\tGPT-4o & 55 & 38 & 17 & 69.1\\% & 0.006 \\\\\n176\tClaude-3.5 & 42 & 22 & 20 & 50.9\\% & 0.880 \\\\\n177\tGemini-2.0 & 48 & 26 & 22 & 53.6\\% & 0.424 \\\\\n178\t\\bottomrule\n179\t\\end{tabular}\n180\t\\end{table}\n181\t\n182\tGPT-4o shows a significant formality preference (69.1\\%, $p=0.006$), while Claude-3.5-Sonnet (50.9\\%, $p=0.88$) and Gemini-2.0-Flash (53.6\\%, $p=0.42$) show no effect.\n183\tThe direction asymmetry in GPT-4o is substantial: when the original is on the formal side, the formal win rate is 78.8\\%; when the original is on the casual side, it drops to 54.5\\%, a 24.2 percentage point gap.\n184\tThis asymmetry foreshadows the original preference confound analyzed in Section~\\ref{sec:original-pref}.\n185\t\n186\tThe cross-judge divergence itself is informative: formality bias is not a universal property of LLM judges but appears to be an artifact of specific RLHF training pipelines.\n187\tClaude-3.5-Sonnet's near-chance performance suggests that Anthropic's training may explicitly optimize for style-agnostic evaluation.\n188\t\n189\tTable~\\ref{tab:cross-benchmark} extends the analysis across benchmarks.\n190\tOf 9 judge-benchmark combinations, only GPT-4o on LLMBar reaches significance.\n191\t\n192\t\\begin{table}[t]\n193\t\\centering\n194\t\\caption{Formality pairwise win rate across judges and benchmarks. Only 1 of 9 combinations is significant ($\\alpha=0.05$).}\n195\t\\label{tab:cross-benchmark}\n196\t\\small\n197\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n198\t\\toprule\n199\t\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{Judge} & \\textbf{$N$} & \\textbf{WR} & \\textbf{$p$} & \\textbf{Sig?} \\\\\n200\t\\midrule\n201\tLLMBar & GPT-4o & 55 & 69.1\\% & 0.006 & Yes \\\\\n202\tLLMBar & Claude-3.5 & 42 & 50.9\\% & 0.880 & No \\\\\n203\tLLMBar & Gemini-2.0 & 48 & 53.6\\% & 0.424 & No \\\\\n204\t\\midrule\n205\tAlpacaEval & GPT-4o & 52 & 54.0\\% & 0.384 & No \\\\\n206\tAlpacaEval & Claude-3.5 & 50 & 48.0\\% & 0.777 & No \\\\\n207\tAlpacaEval & Gemini-2.0 & 47 & 51.1\\% & 0.916 & No \\\\\n208\t\\midrule\n209\tMT-Bench & GPT-4o & 45 & 57.8\\% & 0.183 & No \\\\\n210\tMT-Bench & Claude-3.5 & 43 & 51.6\\% & 0.553 & No \\\\\n211\tMT-Bench & Gemini-2.0 & 44 & 45.5\\% & 0.617 & No \\\\\n212\t\\bottomrule\n213\t\\end{tabular}\n214\t\\end{table}\n215\t\n216\tThe 1/9 significant rate is close to the expected false positive rate under multiple testing.\n217\tUnder Bonferroni correction ($\\alpha' = 0.05/9 = 0.0056$), even the GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar result ($p=0.006$) falls on the boundary.\n218\tGPT-4o's effect attenuates on AlpacaEval (54.0\\%, $p=0.38$) and MT-Bench (57.8\\%, $p=0.18$), where greater content quality variance across responses dilutes the relative influence of style cues.\n219\tNLI pass rates on the new benchmarks remain above 80\\% (AlpacaEval: 82\\%, MT-Bench: 84\\%), confirming that the effect disappearance is genuine rather than an artifact of counterfactual quality.\n220\t\n221\t\\subsection{Register Axis: A Clean Null Result}\n222\t\\label{sec:register}\n223\t\n224\tRegister (academic vs.\\ conversational) passes content verification at the 80\\% NLI threshold.\n225\tTable~\\ref{tab:register} shows that no judge exhibits a significant preference for either register.\n226\t\n227\t\\begin{table}[t]\n228\t\\centering\n229\t\\caption{Register axis pairwise results. Content is preserved but no judge shows a significant register preference.}\n230\t\\label{tab:register}\n231\t\\small\n232\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n233\t\\toprule\n234\t\\textbf{Judge} & \\textbf{NLI Pass} & \\textbf{Academic WR} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n235\t\\midrule\n236\tGPT-4o & 80\\% & 54.0\\% & 0.45 \\\\\n237\tClaude-3.5 & 80\\% & 51.0\\% & 0.87 \\\\\n238\tGemini-2.0 & 80\\% & 49.0\\% & 0.92 \\\\\n239\t\\bottomrule\n240\t\\end{tabular}\n241\t\\end{table}\n242\t\n243\tThis null result serves as a validity check for the CSD framework: the method does not indiscriminately flag every style dimension as biased.\n244\tAmong the two axes that pass content verification (formality and register), only formality shows any signal, and only for one judge on one benchmark.\n245\t\n246\t\\subsection{Likert versus Pairwise: Limited Divergence}\n247\t\\label{sec:likert-pairwise}\n248\t\n249\tTable~\\ref{tab:likert-pairwise} compares Likert average treatment effect (ATE) with pairwise win rate across all 9 judge-benchmark settings on the formality axis.\n250\t\n251\t\\begin{table}[t]\n252\t\\centering\n253\t\\caption{Likert ATE versus pairwise win rate for formality. A ``gap'' denotes qualitative disagreement (one significant, one not). Only 1 of 9 settings shows a gap.}\n254\t\\label{tab:likert-pairwise}\n255\t\\small\n256\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccl}\n257\t\\toprule\n258\t\\textbf{Judge} & \\textbf{Bench} & \\textbf{L-ATE} & \\textbf{L-$p$} & \\textbf{P-WR} & \\textbf{P-$p$} & \\textbf{Gap} \\\\\n259\t\\midrule\n260\tGPT-4o & LLMBar & $-$0.10 & .332 & 69.1\\% & .006 & \\textbf{Yes} \\\\\n261\tGPT-4o & Alpaca & 0.03 & .810 & 54.0\\% & .384 & No \\\\\n262\tGPT-4o & MT-B & 0.09 & .420 & 57.8\\% & .183 & No \\\\\n263\tClaude & LLMBar & 0.02 & .880 & 50.9\\% & .880 & No \\\\\n264\tClaude & Alpaca & $-$0.01 & .940 & 48.0\\% & .777 & No \\\\\n265\tClaude & MT-B & 0.04 & .760 & 51.6\\% & .553 & No \\\\\n266\tGemini & LLMBar & 0.05 & .720 & 53.6\\% & .424 & No \\\\\n267\tGemini & Alpaca & 0.01 & .930 & 51.1\\% & .916 & No \\\\\n268\tGemini & MT-B & $-$0.03 & .820 & 45.5\\% & .617 & No \\\\\n269\t\\bottomrule\n270\t\\end{tabular}\n271\t\\end{table}\n272\t\n273\tThe Likert-pairwise gap appears only for GPT-4o on LLMBar, where the Likert ATE is non-significant ($-$0.10, $p=0.33$) while the pairwise win rate is significant (69.1\\%, $p=0.006$).\n274\tIn all other 8 settings, the two paradigms agree (both non-significant).\n275\tThis prevalence of 1/9 (11\\%) does not support a claim that Likert scoring \\emph{systematically} underestimates style bias.\n276\tInstead, the gap appears to be an interaction between a specific judge's pairwise behavior and a specific benchmark's properties: LLMBar's quality-controlled items leave little content variance, amplifying the relative influence of style cues in forced comparison.\n277\t\n278\tThe practical recommendation is that researchers should report both Likert and pairwise results when a discrepancy could affect conclusions, but the gap is not pervasive enough to invalidate either paradigm in general.\n279\t\n280\t\\subsection{Original Preference: The Hidden Confound}\n281\t\\label{sec:original-pref}\n282\t\n283\tTable~\\ref{tab:original-pref} reports the original preference control experiment.\n284\tJudges compare original text against its same-style double rewrite (no style change, only the rewriting process itself).\n285\t\n286\t\\begin{table}[t]\n287\t\\centering\n288\t\\caption{Original preference: judges compare original text versus its same-style rewrite. All three judges prefer originals.}\n289\t\\label{tab:original-pref}\n290\t\\small\n291\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n292\t\\toprule\n293\t\\textbf{Judge} & \\textbf{$N_\\text{cons}$} & \\textbf{Orig Wins} & \\textbf{Orig WR} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n294\t\\midrule\n295\tGPT-4o & 58 & 38 & 65.5\\% & 0.017 \\\\\n296\tClaude-3.5 & 50 & 31 & 62.0\\% & 0.080 \\\\\n297\tGemini-2.0 & 52 & 31 & 59.6\\% & 0.140 \\\\\n298\t\\bottomrule\n299\t\\end{tabular}\n300\t\\end{table}\n301\t\n302\tGPT-4o significantly prefers originals (65.5\\%, $p=0.017$), while Claude-3.5 (62.0\\%, $p=0.08$) and Gemini-2.0 (59.6\\%, $p=0.14$) show the same directional trend.\n303\tThis means that in any counterfactual comparison where one side is the original text and the other is a rewrite, the original side receives a systematic boost unrelated to style.\n304\t\n305\tTable~\\ref{tab:decomposition} decomposes the GPT-4o formality effect into its components.\n306\t\n307\t\\begin{table}[t]\n308\t\\centering\n309\t\\caption{Decomposition of the GPT-4o formality effect on LLMBar. The raw effect is largely attributable to original preference.}\n310\t\\label{tab:decomposition}\n311\t\\small\n312\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n313\t\\toprule\n314\t\\textbf{Component} & \\textbf{Est.\\ WR} & \\textbf{Note} \\\\\n315\t\\midrule\n316\tRaw formality effect & 69.1\\% & Pairwise, $p=0.006$ \\\\\n317\tOriginal preference & 65.5\\% & Control, $p=0.017$ \\\\\n318\tCorrected formality & $\\sim$53.6\\% & Residual, $p \\approx 0.35$ \\\\\n319\t\\bottomrule\n320\t\\end{tabular}\n321\t\\end{table}\n322\t\n323\tAfter subtracting the original preference component, the residual formality effect drops to approximately 53.6\\% ($p \\approx 0.35$), which is not significant.\n324\tThe direction asymmetry observed earlier (78.8\\% vs.\\ 54.5\\%) is now explained: when the formal version is the original, GPT-4o's preference is boosted by both formality and original preference acting in the same direction; when the formal version is the rewrite, the two forces oppose each other.\n325\t\n326\tThis finding has implications beyond our study.\n327\tAny rewriting-based style bias measurement (including CASSE and SOS-Bench variants that use LLM rewrites) is susceptible to the same confound.\n328\tLLM-generated text, even from strong models, carries subtle distributional signatures that judges can detect.\n329\tWithout an original preference control, it is impossible to attribute observed effects to style rather than to the rewriting process.\n330\t\n331\t\\subsection{Scaled Replication}\n332\t\\label{sec:scaled}\n333\t\n334\tTo rule out power concerns, we scale the GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar formality experiment to $n=500$.\n335\tTable~\\ref{tab:scaled} compares the $n=100$ and $n=500$ results.\n336\t\n337\t\\begin{table}[t]\n338\t\\centering\n339\t\\caption{Scaled replication of GPT-4o formality bias on LLMBar. The raw effect attenuates at larger $n$; the corrected effect remains non-significant.}\n340\t\\label{tab:scaled}\n341\t\\small\n342\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n343\t\\toprule\n344\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{$n=100$} & \\textbf{$n=500$} \\\\\n345\t\\midrule\n346\tFormal win rate & 69.1\\% & 57.1\\% \\\\\n347\tSign test $p$ & 0.006 & 0.021 \\\\\n348\tDirection asymmetry & 24.2\\% & 18.0\\% \\\\\n349\tCorrected WR & --- & 53.7\\% \\\\\n350\tCorrected $p$ & --- & 0.18 \\\\\n351\t95\\% CI & [56.0, 79.7] & [51.3, 62.7] \\\\\n352\t\\bottomrule\n353\t\\end{tabular}\n354\t\\end{table}\n355\t\n356\tThe raw win rate drops from 69.1\\% to 57.1\\%, consistent with regression to the mean: the $n=100$ estimate was inflated by sampling variance.\n357\tThe raw effect remains nominally significant ($p=0.021$), confirming that \\emph{something} is present.\n358\tAfter original preference correction, the effect falls to 53.7\\% ($p=0.18$), still non-significant.\n359\tPower analysis confirms that at $n=500$, the study has 82\\% power to detect an effect size of $d=0.05$, so the non-significance of the corrected effect is not due to insufficient power.\n360\tThe minimum detectable effect at this sample size is 4.5 percentage points above 50\\%.\n361\t\n362\t\\subsection{Rewriter Ablation}\n363\t\\label{sec:rewriter}\n364\t\n365\tTo verify that the original preference confound is not specific to the Qwen3-32B rewriter, we replicate the GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar experiment using Llama-3.1-70B as an independent rewriter.\n366\t\n367\t\\begin{table}[t]\n368\t\\centering\n369\t\\caption{Rewriter ablation on GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar. Both rewriters produce similar patterns: substantial raw effects that disappear after original preference correction.}\n370\t\\label{tab:rewriter}\n371\t\\small\n372\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n373\t\\toprule\n374\t\\textbf{Rewriter} & \\textbf{NLI} & \\textbf{Raw WR} & \\textbf{Raw $p$} & \\textbf{Orig Pref} & \\textbf{Corr.\\ WR} \\\\\n375\t\\midrule\n376\tQwen3-32B & 88\\% & 69.1\\% & 0.006 & 65.5\\% & $\\sim$53.6\\% \\\\\n377\tLlama-3.1-70B & 83\\% & 63.0\\% & 0.060 & 68.0\\% & $\\sim$50.0\\% \\\\\n378\t\\bottomrule\n379\t\\end{tabular}\n380\t\\end{table}\n381\t\n382\tLlama-3.1-70B produces slightly lower-quality counterfactuals (NLI 83\\% vs.\\ 88\\%) and a correspondingly weaker raw formality effect (63.0\\%, $p=0.06$).\n383\tCritically, the original preference is \\emph{higher} for Llama rewrites (68.0\\% vs.\\ 65.5\\%), consistent with slightly more detectable distributional differences in Llama's output.\n384\tAfter correction, the formality effect with Llama rewrites is 50.0\\%, exactly at chance.\n385\tThis convergent evidence from two independent rewriters confirms that the observed formality signal is predominantly a rewriting artifact rather than a genuine style preference.\n386\t\n387\t% ===========================================================================\n388\t\\section{Discussion}\n389\t\n390\t\\paragraph{What do these negative results mean?}\n391\tThe absence of robust style bias after proper controls does not mean LLM judges are unbiased.\n392\tIt means that the specific counterfactual rewriting methodology we tested (and that underlies several recent publications) cannot reliably isolate style effects from rewriting artifacts.\n393\tJudges may well have style preferences, but measuring them requires methods that do not introduce systematic confounds.\n394\t\n395\t\\paragraph{Why does original preference exist?}\n396\tLLM-generated rewrites, even when semantically faithful, differ from human-written originals in subtle distributional ways: token frequency patterns, syntactic diversity, discourse structure.\n397\tJudges trained on large corpora of human text may implicitly detect and penalize these artifacts.\n398\tThe 60--68\\% original preference rates we observe suggest this effect is substantial and consistent across judge families.\n399\t\n400\t\\paragraph{Implications for the style bias literature.}\n401\tStudies that use LLM rewriting to generate style variants without controlling for original preference may report inflated or spurious effects.\n402\tThis applies to any study comparing original text against its style-modified rewrite, including verbosity, tone, and formality manipulations.\n403\tWe recommend that future rewriting-based bias studies include an original preference control as standard practice.\n404\t\n405\t\\paragraph{Content preservation as a prerequisite.}\n406\tThe failure of verbosity and tone axes to pass content verification highlights an underappreciated challenge.\n407\t``Style'' and ``content'' are not always cleanly separable: changing verbosity changes information density, and changing tone changes epistemic commitment.\n408\tOnly style dimensions that can be manipulated without altering propositional content are amenable to counterfactual bias measurement.\n409\tThis imposes a fundamental limit on the scope of rewriting-based approaches.\n410\t\n411\t\\paragraph{Benchmark properties as a moderator.}\n412\tLLMBar's quality-controlled design, where responses are carefully matched in content quality, may amplify the relative influence of surface features.\n413\tOn AlpacaEval and MT-Bench, where responses vary more in content quality, style cues become secondary.\n414\tThis suggests that style bias may be most consequential in settings where content differences are small, precisely the settings where fair evaluation matters most.\n415\t\n416\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n417\tOur study has several limitations.\n418\tFirst, we test only four style axes; other dimensions (e.g., formatting, code style) may behave differently.\n419\tSecond, our original preference correction is approximate; a more principled decomposition would require a formal causal model.\n420\tThird, the content verification thresholds (80\\% NLI pass rate) are somewhat arbitrary, and different thresholds would yield different boundary decisions.\n421\tFourth, our sample sizes, while adequate for detecting medium effects, may miss very small but real biases.\n422\t\n423\t% ===========================================================================\n424\t\\section{Conclusion}\n425\t\n426\tWe set out to build a causal framework for measuring LLM judge style biases and instead discovered fundamental challenges in the rewriting-based measurement approach itself.\n427\tTwo of four tested style axes fail content preservation, the sole significant finding (GPT-4o formality bias) is largely attributable to judges' preference for original over rewritten text, and the Likert-pairwise measurement gap is too rare to constitute a systematic finding.\n428\tThese negative results carry a constructive message for the community: rewriting-based style bias studies need both content preservation verification and original preference controls to produce valid conclusions.\n429\tWe release our CSD framework, including the verification pipeline and control experiment protocol, to support more rigorous style bias measurement in future work.\n430\t\n431\t% ===========================================================================\n432\t\\bibliographystyle{acl_natbib}\n433\t\n434\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n435\t\n436\t\\bibitem[Ouyang et al.(2022)]{ouyang2022training}\n437\tLong Ouyang, Jeffrey Wu, Xu Jiang, et al.\n438\t\\newblock Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback.\n439\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2022.\n440\t\n441\t\\bibitem[{SOS-Bench}(2025)]{sosbench2025}\n442\t{SOS-Bench Authors}.\n443\t\\newblock SOS-Bench: Systematic evaluation of style versus content sensitivity in {LLM} judges.\n444\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2025.\n445\t\n446\t\\bibitem[{Preference Leakage}(2026)]{preferenceleakage2026}\n447\t{Preference Leakage Authors}.\n448\t\\newblock Preference leakage in {LLM}-as-judge: How training data style biases propagate to evaluation.\n449\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2026.\n450\t\n451\t\\bibitem[{CASSE}(2026)]{casse2026}\n452\t{CASSE Authors}.\n453\t\\newblock {CASSE}: Compositional analysis of style sensitivity in evaluation.\n454\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2026.\n455\t\n456\t\\bibitem[{LC-AlpacaEval}(2025)]{lcalpacaeval2025}\n457\t{LC-AlpacaEval Authors}.\n458\t\\newblock Length-controlled {AlpacaEval}: A simple way to debias automatic evaluators.\n459\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2025.\n460\t\n461\t\\bibitem[{LLMBar}(2024)]{llmbar2024}\n462\t{LLMBar Authors}.\n463\t\\newblock {LLMBar}: Adversarial evaluation of {LLM}-as-judge.\n464\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}, 2024.\n465\t\n466\t\\bibitem[Kaushik et al.(2020)]{kaushik2020learning}\n467\tDivyansh Kaushik, Eduard Hovy, and Zachary Lipton.\n468\t\\newblock Learning the difference that makes a difference with counterfactually-augmented data.\n469\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2020.\n470\t\n471\t\\bibitem[Zheng et al.(2023)]{zheng2023judging}\n472\tLianmin Zheng, Wei-Lin Chiang, Ying Sheng, et al.\n473\t\\newblock Judging {LLM}-as-a-judge with {MT-Bench} and {Chatbot Arena}.\n474\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2023.\n475\t\n476\t\\end{thebibliography}\n477\t\n478\t\\end{document}\n479\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_014rPiCMT7MfQUBvGU4srpDr","session_id":"6cdc1f76-5d9c-4713-b8a5-8974082b9307","uuid":"d3cba5e0-1740-481c-8678-d5355413bf7b","timestamp":"2026-05-20T22:03:43.263Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MNTRe5aHNZAnazEM6NC7Wc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UVQZASgaQxEhE3guq8rFgm","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **novelty, significance, and related work positioning**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"How Should We Measure LLM Judge Biases? Lessons from Style Counterfactuals\"\n\nThe paper proposes CSD (Causal Style Decomposition), a framework for measuring LLM judge style biases via counterfactual rewriting + content verification + original preference control. Tests 4 style axes × 3 judges × 3 benchmarks. Key findings are predominantly negative:\n1. Content preservation is binding: 2/4 axes (verbosity, tone) fail NLI verification\n2. Original preference confound: judges prefer originals over rewrites at 60-68%; after correction, the only significant result (GPT-4o formality 69.1%) drops to ~53.6% (non-significant). Replicated at n=500 and with independent rewriter.\n3. Likert-pairwise gap in only 1/9 settings.\n\n## Related Work (as presented in paper)\nThe paper positions against:\n- **SOS-Bench (ICLR 2025)**: style vs content sensitivity via factual error injection, bias score [0.76, 0.92]\n- **Preference Leakage (ICLR 2026)**: style features in training data → preference leakage, R=0.999\n- **CASSE (arXiv 2026)**: 12×12 style feature interaction matrix via prompt engineering\n- **LC-AlpacaEval (ICLR 2025)**: regression-based length decontamination\n- **LLMBar (ACL 2024)**: adversarial perturbations for judge robustness\n- **Kaushik et al. (ICLR 2020)**: counterfactual data augmentation\n- **Zheng et al. (NeurIPS 2023)**: MT-Bench, pairwise vs pointwise\n\nThe paper claims two key differentiators from prior work:\n(a) Uses counterfactual rewriting (not prompt engineering) enabling post-hoc analysis\n(b) Explicitly verifies content preservation and controls for original preference — \"two validity checks absent from prior work\"\n\n## Three Claimed Contributions\n1. Content preservation as a binding constraint (verbosity, tone fail)\n2. Original preference as a universal confound (60-68% across judges)\n3. Systematic Likert vs Pairwise comparison (gap in 1/9)\n\n## Paper Structure\n- 8 pages, 9 tables, no figures in the PDF (though the experiment design mentions several figures)\n- Sections: Intro, Related Work, Method (CSD), Experimental Setup, Results (7 subsections), Discussion, Limitations, Conclusion\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on NOVELTY, SIGNIFICANCE, and RELATED WORK. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Novelty**: How novel is the CSD framework? Is the \"original preference\" finding genuinely new? How much of this is incremental vs. a real conceptual advance?\n2. **Significance/Impact**: Will these findings change how the community does style bias research? Are the negative results impactful enough?\n3. **Related work coverage**: Are important prior works missing? Is the positioning fair to prior work?\n4. **Framing and narrative**: Is the paper well-framed as a methodology contribution? Does the \"negative results\" framing work?\n5. **Broader context**: Does this paper advance the field's understanding, or is it primarily confirmatory/cautionary?\n\nImportant related works the paper should discuss but might be missing:\n- FairEval / Chatbot Arena work on judge calibration\n- Human annotation agreement studies (how do human annotators compare?)\n- Style transfer literature (the content preservation challenge is well-known there)\n- Causal inference methodology beyond basic sign tests\n- Other counterfactual evaluation papers in NLP\n\nStructure your output as:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n(2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n(Numbered list, be specific)\n\n### Weaknesses\n(Numbered list, be specific and cite evidence)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n(Numbered list)\n\n### Minor Issues\n(Numbered list)\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous. A top venue expects novelty and significance beyond \"we tested X and it didn't work.\" Negative results papers need exceptionally strong methodology AND actionable insights to merit acceptance."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":31903,"cache_read_input_tokens":23651,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":31903},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6cdc1f76-5d9c-4713-b8a5-8974082b9307","uuid":"539a5510-af16-4f0a-b789-6d625a6c0021"}
