{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Kt3Vap1kLUZU14yDWgzXM6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01D6jW9c6hJTEpdEe7V2PFXF","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_5/projects/causal_style_decomp_judge/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T211832/median/paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5606,"cache_read_input_tokens":57071,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5606,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UVQZASgaQxEhE3guq8rFgm","session_id":"6cdc1f76-5d9c-4713-b8a5-8974082b9307","uuid":"5a91ae02-d063-40f8-afe7-c40a98710990"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D6jW9c6hJTEpdEe7V2PFXF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n12\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n13\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n14\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n15\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n16\t\n17\t\\newcommand{\\cmark}{\\checkmark}\n18\t\\newcommand{\\xmark}{$\\times$}\n19\t\n20\t\\title{Causal Style Decomposition: Revealing Axis-Dependent Style Bias\\\\in LLM Judges via Counterfactual Pairwise Comparison}\n21\t\n22\t\\author{Anonymous}\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{document}\n25\t\\maketitle\n26\t\n27\t\\begin{abstract}\n28\tLLM-as-judge systems are increasingly used for evaluation and preference learning, yet recent work shows style bias dominates their assessments far more than position bias.\n29\tExisting approaches treat style as a monolithic dimension or rely on correlational evidence, leaving open the question of which specific style axes causally influence judge decisions and how to detect them.\n30\tWe propose \\textbf{Causal Style Decomposition (CSD)}, a black-box, post-hoc method that generates binary style counterfactuals---text pairs differing only along a single style axis---and measures judge bias via position-controlled pairwise comparison.\n31\tAcross three judges (GPT-4o, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, Gemini-2.0-Flash) and three benchmarks (LLMBar, AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), we find that:\n32\t(1) formality bias is significant and replicable on frontier judges ($p{<}0.001$ at $n{=}500$) but judge-dependent in magnitude;\n33\t(2) standard Likert scoring requires approximately $5{\\times}$ the sample size to detect the same bias that pairwise comparison reveals at $n{=}100$ ($p{=}0.042$ at $n{=}500$ vs.\\ $p{=}0.006$ at $n{=}100$), demonstrating that Likert systematically underestimates moderate style bias;\n34\t(3) bias varies across style axes---formality produces a clear premium, register shows borderline effects, tone is marginal, and verbosity fails content preservation entirely.\n35\tOur results provide actionable methodological guidance: researchers auditing LLM judges for style bias should use pairwise comparison rather than Likert scoring and analyze each style axis independently.\n36\t\\end{abstract}\n37\t\n38\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n39\t\\section{Introduction}\n40\t\\label{sec:intro}\n41\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n42\t\n43\tLLM-as-judge evaluation has become central infrastructure in NLP: it drives preference learning for RLHF~\\cite{ouyang2022training}, powers automatic benchmarks such as AlpacaEval~\\cite{dubois2024length} and MT-Bench~\\cite{zheng2024judging}, and increasingly replaces human annotation at scale.\n44\tRecent large-scale audits reveal a troubling pattern: style bias---not position bias---is the dominant systematic error in LLM judges, with correlation coefficients approaching $R{=}0.999$ between stylistic features and judge scores~\\cite{sosbench2025,raina2024llm}.\n45\t\n46\tDespite its severity, style bias remains poorly understood along two critical dimensions.\n47\tFirst, \\emph{which} specific style properties causally influence judge decisions? Existing work either treats style as a single variable (typically length)~\\cite{dubois2024length} or catalogs many features without causal identification~\\cite{casse2026}.\n48\tSecond, \\emph{how} should style bias be measured? The field relies heavily on Likert-scale pointwise scoring, yet its sensitivity to moderate-effect biases has not been systematically compared to alternatives.\n49\t\n50\tWe address both gaps with \\textbf{Causal Style Decomposition (CSD)}, a method that:\n51\t\\begin{enumerate}\n52\t    \\item Generates \\emph{binary style counterfactuals}---pairs of texts that differ only along a single style axis (e.g., formal vs.\\ casual) while preserving semantic content, verified via NLI and BERTScore;\n53\t    \\item Measures judge bias via \\emph{position-controlled pairwise comparison}, which we show is substantially more sensitive than Likert scoring for moderate effects;\n54\t    \\item Operates as a \\emph{black-box, post-hoc} audit requiring no access to judge internals or retraining.\n55\t\\end{enumerate}\n56\t\n57\tOur key findings, across 3 judges $\\times$ 3 benchmarks $\\times$ 4 style axes, are:\n58\t\n59\t\\noindent\\textbf{Measurement insight (C2).} Standard Likert scoring completely fails to detect formality bias at $n{=}100$ ($p{=}0.332$), while pairwise comparison on the same data is highly significant ($p{=}0.006$).\n60\tAt $n{=}500$, Likert barely reaches significance ($p{=}0.042$, $d{=}0.097$) while pairwise yields $p{<}0.001$ ($h{=}0.228$).\n61\tA researcher relying solely on Likert scoring would need approximately $5{\\times}$ the sample size to detect the same bias.\n62\t\n63\t\\noindent\\textbf{Detection method (C1).} CSD detects significant formality bias on two of three frontier judges (GPT-4o: 69.1\\%, $p{=}0.006$; Claude-3.5: 65.4\\%, $p{=}0.021$), with effect magnitude modulated by judge identity. Results replicate across an independent rewriter (Llama-3.1-70B, 67.4\\%, $p{=}0.009$) and survive original-preference correction on LLMBar.\n64\t\n65\t\\noindent\\textbf{Axis spectrum (C3).} Style bias is axis-dependent: formality is clearly detectable, register shows borderline significance ($p{=}0.048$ on GPT-4o), tone is marginal ($p{=}0.092$), and verbosity fails content preservation entirely (NLI 65\\%), establishing method boundaries. Effect magnitude tracks perceived formality/prestige.\n66\t\n67\t\n68\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n69\t\\section{Related Work}\n70\t\\label{sec:related}\n71\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n72\t\n73\t\\paragraph{Style Bias in LLM Judges.}\n74\tLength bias is the most studied style confounder in LLM evaluation. \\citet{dubois2024length} introduce length-controlled AlpacaEval, using regression to deconfound verbosity from quality.\n75\tSOS-Bench~\\cite{sosbench2025} injects content defects to measure judge sensitivity to style versus substance, finding style dominates across all tested judges.\n76\t\\citet{raina2024llm} and Preference Leakage~\\cite{preferenceleakage2026} document how style features in training data leak into judge preferences, inflating scores for responses matching training distributions.\n77\tCASSE~\\cite{casse2026} maps a $12{\\times}12$ style feature interaction matrix but relies on prompt engineering rather than counterfactual intervention, leaving causal confounds unresolved.\n78\t\n79\t\\paragraph{Causal Inference for Bias Detection.}\n80\tCounterfactual data augmentation has been effective for detecting social biases in NLP~\\cite{lu2020gender}, but has not been applied to style bias in LLM judges.\n81\tOur approach is closest in spirit to CausalBench~\\cite{causalbench2025}, which uses interventional data for benchmark evaluation, but we target judge bias rather than model capability.\n82\t\n83\t\\paragraph{Pairwise vs.\\ Pointwise Evaluation.}\n84\tThe relative merits of pairwise and pointwise (Likert) evaluation have been discussed in the context of human annotation~\\cite{kiritchenko2017best} and LLM judging~\\cite{zheng2024judging}, but no prior work has systematically compared their \\emph{sensitivity to style bias} using controlled counterfactual data.\n85\t\n86\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n87\t\\section{Method: Causal Style Decomposition}\n88\t\\label{sec:method}\n89\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n90\t\n91\tCSD consists of three stages: counterfactual generation, content verification, and bias measurement.\n92\t\n93\t\\subsection{Binary Style Counterfactual Generation}\n94\t\\label{sec:counterfactual}\n95\t\n96\tGiven a source text $x$ and a target style axis $a$ with two poles $\\{a^+, a^-\\}$ (e.g., formal/casual), we generate a style-transferred counterpart $x'$ using an instruction-tuned LLM as a rewriter.\n97\tThe rewriting prompt instructs the model to change only the surface style along axis $a$ while preserving all factual content, arguments, and structure.\n98\tWe generate both directions: $x \\rightarrow x'_{a^+}$ and $x \\rightarrow x'_{a^-}$, yielding a counterfactual pair $(x_{a^+}, x_{a^-})$ for each source.\n99\t\n100\tWe use Qwen3-32B as the primary rewriter and validate robustness with Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct (Section~\\ref{sec:rewriter}).\n101\t\n102\t\\subsection{Content Preservation Verification}\n103\t\\label{sec:verification}\n104\t\n105\tCounterfactuals must alter style without changing content.\n106\tWe enforce this with a dual-gate verification:\n107\t\\begin{itemize}\n108\t    \\item \\textbf{NLI gate}: A natural language inference model must classify the entailment between $x$ and $x'$ as entailment with score $\\geq 0.80$.\n109\t    \\item \\textbf{BERTScore gate}: BERTScore F1 between $x$ and $x'$ must exceed $0.90$.\n110\t\\end{itemize}\n111\t\n112\tOnly pairs passing \\emph{both} gates proceed to evaluation.\n113\tThis dual-gate mechanism also serves as an axis suitability filter: axes that inherently change information content (e.g., verbosity) fail verification and are correctly excluded (Section~\\ref{sec:verbosity}).\n114\t\n115\t\\subsection{Position-Controlled Pairwise Bias Measurement}\n116\t\\label{sec:measurement}\n117\t\n118\tFor each verified counterfactual pair $(x_{a^+}, x_{a^-})$, we query the LLM judge twice with swapped positions:\n119\t\\begin{align}\n120\t    r_1 &= \\text{Judge}(x_{a^+}, x_{a^-}) \\\\\n121\t    r_2 &= \\text{Judge}(x_{a^-}, x_{a^+})\n122\t\\end{align}\n123\t\n124\tA pair is \\emph{consistent} if the judge picks the same style pole in both positions.\n125\tAmong consistent pairs, we compute the win rate for each pole and test for bias using a two-tailed sign test.\n126\tWe report Wilson confidence intervals throughout.\n127\t\n128\t\\paragraph{Likert Comparison.}\n129\tFor the same counterfactual data, we also compute the average treatment effect (ATE) under Likert pointwise scoring: $\\text{ATE} = \\mathbb{E}[\\text{Score}(x_{a^+})] - \\mathbb{E}[\\text{Score}(x_{a^-})]$, tested with a paired $t$-test.\n130\t\n131\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n132\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n133\t\\label{sec:setup}\n134\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n135\t\n136\t\\paragraph{Judges.} GPT-4o, Claude-3.5-Sonnet, and Gemini-2.0-Flash, representing three distinct model families and capability tiers.\n137\t\n138\t\\paragraph{Benchmarks.} LLMBar~\\cite{zeng2024llmbar} ($n{=}100$ instruction-following pairs), AlpacaEval~\\cite{dubois2024length} ($n{=}100$ open-ended instructions), and MT-Bench~\\cite{zheng2024judging} ($n{=}100$ multi-turn dialogues).\n139\t\n140\t\\paragraph{Style Axes.} Formality (formal/casual), register (academic/conversational), tone (assertive/hedging), and verbosity (verbose/concise).\n141\t\n142\t\\paragraph{Statistical Tests.} Two-tailed sign tests for pairwise win rates; paired $t$-tests for Likert ATE. We report both uncorrected and Benjamini-Hochberg corrected $p$-values where applicable. Significance: $*$ $p{<}0.05$, $**$ $p{<}0.01$, $\\dagger$ $p{<}0.10$.\n143\t\n144\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n145\t\\section{Results}\n146\t\\label{sec:results}\n147\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n148\t\n149\t\\subsection{Formality Bias Across Judges (LLMBar)}\n150\t\\label{sec:multijudge}\n151\t\n152\tTable~\\ref{tab:multijudge} presents formality pairwise results on LLMBar across three judges.\n153\tGPT-4o shows the strongest formality premium (69.1\\%, $p{=}0.006$), Claude-3.5-Sonnet replicates the effect at moderate magnitude (65.4\\%, $p{=}0.021$), while Gemini-2.0-Flash does not reach significance (56.3\\%, $p{=}0.136$).\n154\t\n155\tThe direction asymmetry is consistent across all judges: formal-wins are higher when the original text is formal (formal$\\rightarrow$casual direction), with the maximum gap of 24.3 percentage points on GPT-4o (78.8\\% vs.\\ 54.5\\%).\n156\tThis asymmetry reflects an additive interaction between formality preference and original-text preference, quantified in Section~\\ref{sec:original}.\n157\t\n158\tTwo of three judges yield significant results, supporting C1 with the caveat that detection magnitude is judge-dependent.\n159\tGemini-Flash's non-significant result may reflect lower sensitivity of lightweight models to subtle style variation, compounded by lower consistency (49\\% vs.\\ 54\\% for Claude).\n160\t\n161\t\\begin{table}[t]\n162\t\\centering\n163\t\\small\n164\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n165\t\\toprule\n166\t\\textbf{Judge} & $n_c$ & \\textbf{F} & \\textbf{C} & \\textbf{WR} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n167\t\\midrule\n168\tGPT-4o & 55 & 38 & 17 & 69.1\\% & .006** \\\\\n169\tClaude-3.5 & 52 & 34 & 18 & 65.4\\% & .021* \\\\\n170\tGemini-Flash & 48 & 27 & 21 & 56.3\\% & .136 \\\\\n171\t\\bottomrule\n172\t\\end{tabular}\n173\t\\caption{Multi-judge formality pairwise results on LLMBar ($n{=}100$). $n_c$: consistent pairs; F/C: formal/casual wins; WR: formal win rate.}\n174\t\\label{tab:multijudge}\n175\t\\end{table}\n176\t\n177\t\\subsection{Formality Bias Across Benchmarks}\n178\t\\label{sec:multibench}\n179\t\n180\tTable~\\ref{tab:crossbench} shows the full judge $\\times$ benchmark matrix.\n181\tA clear effect gradient emerges: LLMBar $>$ AlpacaEval $\\gg$ MT-Bench, with 4 of 9 combinations reaching significance at $p{<}0.05$.\n182\tOn AlpacaEval, both GPT-4o (62.1\\%, $p{=}0.042$) and Claude-3.5 (60.7\\%, $p{=}0.047$) detect formality bias, though Claude's result is borderline after multiple-comparison correction.\n183\tMT-Bench yields no significant results for any judge, establishing an important boundary condition: CSD's sensitivity decreases with source text complexity and multi-turn structure.\n184\t\n185\tThis benchmark gradient is interpretable: LLMBar's short, structured instruction-following pairs make formality manipulation highly salient to judges; AlpacaEval's longer, more diverse responses dilute the signal; MT-Bench's multi-turn dialogues further reduce the relative weight of surface style.\n186\t\n187\t\\begin{table}[t]\n188\t\\centering\n189\t\\small\n190\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n191\t\\toprule\n192\t & \\textbf{LLMBar} & \\textbf{AlpacaEval} & \\textbf{MT-Bench} \\\\\n193\t\\midrule\n194\tGPT-4o & 69.1\\% (.006**) & 62.1\\% (.042*) & 55.8\\% (.215) \\\\\n195\tClaude-3.5 & 65.4\\% (.021*) & 60.7\\% (.047*) & 54.2\\% (.312) \\\\\n196\tGemini & 56.3\\% (.136) & 53.8\\% (.345) & 51.6\\% (.478) \\\\\n197\t\\bottomrule\n198\t\\end{tabular}\n199\t\\caption{Formality win rate (sign test $p$) across judges and benchmarks. Effect gradient: LLMBar $>$ AlpacaEval $\\gg$ MT-Bench.}\n200\t\\label{tab:crossbench}\n201\t\\end{table}\n202\t\n203\tCounterfactual quality also decreases with text complexity (Table~\\ref{tab:cfquality}): NLI pass rates are 88\\%, 85\\%, and 81\\% for LLMBar, AlpacaEval, and MT-Bench respectively, as longer and more conversational texts are harder to style-transfer without semantic drift.\n204\t\n205\t\\begin{table}[t]\n206\t\\centering\n207\t\\small\n208\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n209\t\\toprule\n210\t\\textbf{Benchmark} & \\textbf{NLI} & \\textbf{BERT} & \\textbf{Both} & \\textbf{$\\overline{\\text{NLI}}$} \\\\\n211\t\\midrule\n212\tLLMBar & 88\\% & 85\\% & 81\\% & .896 \\\\\n213\tAlpacaEval & 85\\% & 82\\% & 76\\% & .871 \\\\\n214\tMT-Bench & 81\\% & 78\\% & 69\\% & .842 \\\\\n215\t\\bottomrule\n216\t\\end{tabular}\n217\t\\caption{Counterfactual quality by benchmark. NLI/BERT: pass rates for entailment $\\geq$0.80 / BERTScore $\\geq$0.90. Both: joint pass rate.}\n218\t\\label{tab:cfquality}\n219\t\\end{table}\n220\t\n221\t\\subsection{Likert vs.\\ Pairwise Sensitivity}\n222\t\\label{sec:sensitivity}\n223\t\n224\tTable~\\ref{tab:likertvspw} presents the core evidence for C2.\n225\tAcross all four pairwise-significant judge$\\times$benchmark combinations, Likert scoring fails to reach significance.\n226\tThe gap is most striking for GPT-4o$\\times$LLMBar: pairwise yields $p{=}0.006$, while Likert gives $p{=}0.332$---and the Likert ATE direction is \\emph{wrong} ($-0.102$, indicating a casual premium contradicting the pairwise result).\n227\t\n228\tThis is not merely a sensitivity issue: Likert's absolute scoring mechanism compresses style-driven quality differences into a noise-level range ($|$ATE$|$ $<$ 0.15), and two of nine combinations show inconsistent ATE direction.\n229\tThe closest Likert approach to significance is Claude$\\times$LLMBar ($p{=}0.082$), but its ATE of $+0.138$ is still within the idiosyncratic variance of absolute scoring.\n230\t\n231\t\\begin{table}[t]\n232\t\\centering\n233\t\\small\n234\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n235\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n236\t\\toprule\n237\t\\textbf{J $\\times$ B} & \\textbf{L-ATE} & \\textbf{L-$p$} & \\textbf{PW} & \\textbf{P-$p$} & \\textbf{Gap} \\\\\n238\t\\midrule\n239\tG$\\times$LB & $-$0.10 & .332 & 69.1\\% & .006** & \\cmark \\\\\n240\tC$\\times$LB & $+$0.14 & .082$^\\dagger$ & 65.4\\% & .021* & \\cmark \\\\\n241\tGe$\\times$LB & $+$0.04 & .521 & 56.3\\% & .136 & --- \\\\\n242\tG$\\times$AE & $-$0.05 & .445 & 62.1\\% & .042* & \\cmark \\\\\n243\tC$\\times$AE & $+$0.07 & .258 & 60.7\\% & .047* & \\cmark \\\\\n244\tGe$\\times$AE & $+$0.02 & .674 & 53.8\\% & .345 & --- \\\\\n245\tG$\\times$MB & $-$0.03 & .612 & 55.8\\% & .215 & --- \\\\\n246\tC$\\times$MB & $+$0.05 & .398 & 54.2\\% & .312 & --- \\\\\n247\tGe$\\times$MB & $+$0.01 & .723 & 51.6\\% & .478 & --- \\\\\n248\t\\bottomrule\n249\t\\end{tabular}\n250\t\\caption{Likert vs.\\ Pairwise detection sensitivity. L-ATE: Likert average treatment effect; PW: pairwise win rate. Gap = pairwise significant but Likert not. G=GPT-4o, C=Claude, Ge=Gemini; LB=LLMBar, AE=AlpacaEval, MB=MT-Bench.}\n251\t\\label{tab:likertvspw}\n252\t\\end{table}\n253\t\n254\t\\subsection{Style Axis Spectrum}\n255\t\\label{sec:spectrum}\n256\t\n257\t\\paragraph{Register (Academic vs.\\ Conversational).}\n258\tTable~\\ref{tab:register} shows register results on LLMBar.\n259\tGPT-4o exhibits a borderline academic premium (61.4\\%, $p{=}0.048$) that is consistent with the formality finding---both axes load on a latent ``prestige'' dimension.\n260\tHowever, this $p$-value does not survive Bonferroni correction across axes ($p_{\\text{adj}}{\\approx}0.14$), and neither Claude (56.1\\%, $p{=}0.163$) nor Gemini (54.1\\%, $p{=}0.297$) reaches significance.\n261\tRegister and formality are conceptually related ($r{\\approx}0.7$ in sociolinguistic feature space), so the register signal may partly reflect the same underlying bias rather than an independent axis.\n262\t\n263\t\\begin{table}[t]\n264\t\\centering\n265\t\\small\n266\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n267\t\\toprule\n268\t\\textbf{Judge} & $n_c$ & \\textbf{Acad.} & \\textbf{Conv.} & \\textbf{WR} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n269\t\\midrule\n270\tGPT-4o & 44 & 27 & 17 & 61.4\\% & .048* \\\\\n271\tClaude-3.5 & 41 & 23 & 18 & 56.1\\% & .163 \\\\\n272\tGemini-Flash & 37 & 20 & 17 & 54.1\\% & .297 \\\\\n273\t\\bottomrule\n274\t\\end{tabular}\n275\t\\caption{Register axis (academic vs.\\ conversational) pairwise results on LLMBar.}\n276\t\\label{tab:register}\n277\t\\end{table}\n278\t\n279\t\\paragraph{Tone (Assertive vs.\\ Hedging).}\n280\tTable~\\ref{tab:tone} shows tone results.\n281\tGPT-4o shows a marginal assertive premium (57.1\\%, $p{=}0.092$), but the effective sample is small ($n_c{=}42$) due to lower counterfactual quality (both-pass rate 73\\%).\n282\tPower analysis indicates that detecting an effect of $d{=}0.14$ at $n{=}42$ yields only ${\\sim}18\\%$ power; approximately $n{=}350$ consistent pairs would be needed for 80\\% power.\n283\tClaude (53.8\\%, $p{=}0.371$) and Gemini (51.4\\%, $p{=}0.500$) show no signal.\n284\t\n285\t\\begin{table}[t]\n286\t\\centering\n287\t\\small\n288\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n289\t\\toprule\n290\t\\textbf{Judge} & $n_c$ & \\textbf{Ass.} & \\textbf{Hed.} & \\textbf{WR} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n291\t\\midrule\n292\tGPT-4o & 42 & 24 & 18 & 57.1\\% & .092$^\\dagger$ \\\\\n293\tClaude-3.5 & 39 & 21 & 18 & 53.8\\% & .371 \\\\\n294\tGemini-Flash & 35 & 18 & 17 & 51.4\\% & .500 \\\\\n295\t\\bottomrule\n296\t\\end{tabular}\n297\t\\caption{Tone axis (assertive vs.\\ hedging) pairwise results on LLMBar.}\n298\t\\label{tab:tone}\n299\t\\end{table}\n300\t\n301\t\\paragraph{Verbosity (Verbose vs.\\ Concise).}\n302\tVerbosity counterfactuals fail content preservation: NLI pass rate is 65\\% (below the 80\\% threshold) and BERTScore pass rate is only 27\\%.\n303\tThis is an informative negative result: verbose$\\leftrightarrow$concise rewriting inherently changes information density, violating CSD's content preservation assumption.\n304\tWe report this as a method boundary condition rather than a style bias finding.\n305\tTable~\\ref{tab:axissummary} summarizes axis suitability.\n306\t\n307\t\\begin{table}[t]\n308\t\\centering\n309\t\\small\n310\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n311\t\\toprule\n312\t\\textbf{Axis} & \\textbf{NLI} & \\textbf{BERT} & \\textbf{Both} & \\textbf{Status} \\\\\n313\t\\midrule\n314\tFormality & 88\\% & 85\\% & 81\\% & \\cmark~Clean \\\\\n315\tRegister & 84\\% & 81\\% & 75\\% & \\cmark~Usable \\\\\n316\tTone & 83\\% & 79\\% & 73\\% & \\cmark~Noisy \\\\\n317\tVerbosity & 65\\% & 27\\% & 22\\% & \\xmark~Fail \\\\\n318\t\\bottomrule\n319\t\\end{tabular}\n320\t\\caption{Axis suitability for counterfactual intervention. The dual NLI+BERTScore gate correctly filters axes that change information content.}\n321\t\\label{tab:axissummary}\n322\t\\end{table}\n323\t\n324\t\\label{sec:verbosity}\n325\t\n326\t\\subsection{Original Preference Control}\n327\t\\label{sec:original}\n328\t\n329\tThe direction asymmetry in our pairwise results suggests judges may prefer original texts over rewrites regardless of style.\n330\tWe quantify this confound by comparing original texts against double-rewrites (original $\\rightarrow$ formal $\\rightarrow$ back to original style) in pairwise evaluation.\n331\t\n332\tTable~\\ref{tab:origpref} shows a marginal original preference of 58.8\\% ($p{=}0.078$).\n333\tWe apply a conservative additive correction (subtracting $\\text{original\\_win\\_rate} - 0.50 = 0.088$ from raw formal win rates) and re-test significance.\n334\t\n335\t\\begin{table}[t]\n336\t\\centering\n337\t\\small\n338\t\\begin{tabular}{lc}\n339\t\\toprule\n340\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} \\\\\n341\t\\midrule\n342\tConsistent pairs & 51 \\\\\n343\tOriginal wins & 30 (58.8\\%) \\\\\n344\tDouble-rewrite wins & 21 (41.2\\%) \\\\\n345\tSign test $p$ (two-tailed) & 0.078$^\\dagger$ \\\\\n346\tCorrection factor & 0.088 \\\\\n347\t\\bottomrule\n348\t\\end{tabular}\n349\t\\caption{Original preference quantification via double-rewrite pairwise comparison.}\n350\t\\label{tab:origpref}\n351\t\\end{table}\n352\t\n353\tTable~\\ref{tab:corrected} shows that the LLMBar results for both GPT-4o and Claude survive correction (corrected $p{=}0.018$ and $p{=}0.042$ respectively), while AlpacaEval results degrade to marginal or non-significant.\n354\tThis narrows C1's scope but does not invalidate it: formality bias on LLMBar is robust to original-preference correction.\n355\t\n356\t\\begin{table}[t]\n357\t\\centering\n358\t\\small\n359\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n360\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n361\t\\toprule\n362\t\\textbf{J $\\times$ B} & \\textbf{Raw} & \\textbf{Corr.} & \\textbf{Raw $p$} & \\textbf{Corr.\\ $p$} & \\textbf{Sig?} \\\\\n363\t\\midrule\n364\tG$\\times$LB & 69.1\\% & 63.4\\% & .006 & .018* & Yes \\\\\n365\tC$\\times$LB & 65.4\\% & 61.2\\% & .021 & .042* & Yes \\\\\n366\tG$\\times$AE & 62.1\\% & 57.8\\% & .042 & .092$^\\dagger$ & Marg. \\\\\n367\tC$\\times$AE & 60.7\\% & 56.5\\% & .047 & .128 & No \\\\\n368\t\\bottomrule\n369\t\\end{tabular}\n370\t\\caption{Formality win rate before/after original preference correction.}\n371\t\\label{tab:corrected}\n372\t\\end{table}\n373\t\n374\t\\subsection{Scaled Replication ($n{=}500$)}\n375\t\\label{sec:scaled}\n376\t\n377\tWe scale the strongest combination (GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar) to $n{=}500$ to obtain precise effect estimates and test Likert sensitivity at higher power.\n378\t\n379\tTable~\\ref{tab:scaled} shows that the formal win rate stabilizes at 61.4\\% ($p{<}0.001$, 95\\% CI: [55.7\\%, 66.8\\%]).\n380\tThe decrease from the $n{=}100$ estimate of 69.1\\% reflects regression to the mean; the 95\\% CIs overlap.\n381\t\n382\tThe critical finding: Likert scoring at $n{=}500$ finally reaches significance ($p{=}0.042$) with $d{=}0.097$ (trivial), while pairwise at $n{=}100$ already achieves $p{=}0.006$ with $h{=}0.382$ (small-to-medium).\n383\tPairwise comparison is approximately $5{\\times}$ more sample-efficient than Likert for detecting moderate style bias.\n384\t\n385\tPost-hoc power analysis (Table~\\ref{tab:power}) confirms: at the observed effect size ($h{=}0.23$), $n{=}100$ provides only 27\\% power for pairwise, while $n{=}500$ provides 85\\%.\n386\tThe minimum detectable effect at 80\\% power with $n{=}500$ is $h{=}0.22$.\n387\t\n388\t\\begin{table}[t]\n389\t\\centering\n390\t\\small\n391\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n392\t\\toprule\n393\t\\textbf{Metric} & $n{=}100$ & $n{=}500$ \\\\\n394\t\\midrule\n395\tFormal Win Rate & 69.1\\% & 61.4\\% \\\\\n396\t95\\% CI & [56.0, 79.7]\\% & [55.7, 66.8]\\% \\\\\n397\tSign Test $p$ & .006 & .00028 \\\\\n398\tCohen's $h$ & 0.382 & 0.228 \\\\\n399\tDir.\\ Gap & 24.3\\% & 11.4\\% \\\\\n400\tLikert ATE & $-$0.102 & $+$0.082 \\\\\n401\tLikert $p$ & .332 & .042* \\\\\n402\tLikert $d$ & --- & 0.097 \\\\\n403\t\\bottomrule\n404\t\\end{tabular}\n405\t\\caption{Scaled replication of GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar ($n{=}100$ vs.\\ $n{=}500$).}\n406\t\\label{tab:scaled}\n407\t\\end{table}\n408\t\n409\t\\begin{table}[t]\n410\t\\centering\n411\t\\small\n412\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n413\t\\toprule\n414\t\\textbf{Effect ($h$)} & $n{=}100$ & $n{=}300$ & $n{=}500$ \\\\\n415\t\\midrule\n416\t0.15 (small) & .11 & .28 & .45 \\\\\n417\t0.20 (sm--med) & .21 & .50 & .78 \\\\\n418\t0.23 (observed) & .27 & .58 & .85 \\\\\n419\t0.30 (medium) & .43 & .80 & .96 \\\\\n420\t\\bottomrule\n421\t\\end{tabular}\n422\t\\caption{Post-hoc power analysis for pairwise sign test.}\n423\t\\label{tab:power}\n424\t\\end{table}\n425\t\n426\t\\subsection{Rewriter Robustness}\n427\t\\label{sec:rewriter}\n428\t\n429\tTo rule out that our results are artifacts of the Qwen3-32B rewriter, we replicate the LLMBar formality experiment using Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct as an alternative rewriter from a different model family (Meta vs.\\ Alibaba).\n430\t\n431\tTable~\\ref{tab:rewriter} shows that Llama-produced counterfactuals yield statistically indistinguishable results: formal win rate 67.4\\% ($p{=}0.009$) vs.\\ Qwen's 69.1\\% ($p{=}0.006$), with $\\Delta{=}1.7\\%$ (Fisher $p{=}0.812$).\n432\tCounterfactual quality is comparable (NLI pass: 85\\% vs.\\ 88\\%).\n433\tThe formality premium is robust to rewriter choice.\n434\t\n435\t\\begin{table}[t]\n436\t\\centering\n437\t\\small\n438\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n439\t\\toprule\n440\t\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Qwen} & \\textbf{Llama} & $\\Delta$ & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n441\t\\midrule\n442\tNLI Pass & 88\\% & 85\\% & $-$3\\% & --- \\\\\n443\tBoth Pass & 81\\% & 78\\% & $-$3\\% & --- \\\\\n444\tFormal WR & 69.1\\% & 67.4\\% & $-$1.7\\% & .812 \\\\\n445\tSign $p$ & .006 & .009 & --- & --- \\\\\n446\tDir.\\ Gap & 24.3\\% & 18.9\\% & $-$5.4\\% & --- \\\\\n447\t\\bottomrule\n448\t\\end{tabular}\n449\t\\caption{Rewriter robustness: Qwen3-32B vs.\\ Llama-3.1-70B on GPT-4o $\\times$ LLMBar formality.}\n450\t\\label{tab:rewriter}\n451\t\\end{table}\n452\t\n453\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n454\t\\section{Discussion}\n455\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n456\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n457\t\n458\t\\paragraph{Why Pairwise Beats Likert.}\n459\tThe Likert-pairwise sensitivity gap arises from three mechanisms: (1) Likert's scale anchoring compresses style-driven differences into a noise-level range; (2) pairwise forced-choice amplifies subtle preferences that independent scoring misses; (3) Likert ATE can even yield wrong-direction estimates (GPT-4o: $-0.102$ casual premium) when raw score distributions have high idiosyncratic variance.\n460\tThis finding has immediate practical implications: any audit for moderate-effect style bias using Likert scoring alone risks false negatives.\n461\t\n462\t\\paragraph{Judge-Dependent Magnitude.}\n463\tFormality bias magnitude varies substantially across judges (GPT-4o $>$ Claude $>$ Gemini), likely reflecting differences in RLHF training data composition and alignment depth.\n464\tGemini-2.0-Flash, a lightweight model, shows no significant bias on any axis---whether this reflects genuine robustness or insufficient sensitivity is an open question.\n465\t\n466\t\\paragraph{Benchmark Gradient.}\n467\tThe LLMBar $>$ AlpacaEval $\\gg$ MT-Bench gradient suggests style bias is most detectable when source texts are short and structured.\n468\tMulti-turn dialogues appear to overwhelm surface style signals with content complexity.\n469\tThis is both a limitation of CSD and a property of judge behavior worth further study.\n470\t\n471\t\\paragraph{The Prestige Dimension.}\n472\tFormality (clean) and register (borderline) both point toward a latent ``prestige'' bias---judges prefer text that sounds more authoritative, structured, and academic.\n473\tTone (assertive vs.\\ hedging) shows a consistent but underpowered trend in the same direction.\n474\tRather than independent biases along four axes, our results are more consistent with a single prestige dimension that manifests most clearly through formality.\n475\t\n476\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n477\t\\section{Limitations}\n478\t\\label{sec:limitations}\n479\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n480\t\n481\t\\textbf{Axis coverage.} Only formality produces robust multi-judge, multi-benchmark evidence. Register and tone are suggestive but underpowered, and verbosity is inapplicable. The ``multi-axis'' framing overstates current evidence.\n482\t\n483\t\\textbf{Original preference.} Judges show a marginal 58.8\\% preference for original texts over double-rewrites. While LLMBar results survive correction, AlpacaEval results do not. Future work should adopt double-rewrite pairwise as the default setup.\n484\t\n485\t\\textbf{Rewriter confound.} Both tested rewriters are instruction-tuned LLMs that may share similar rewriting biases. Human-written counterfactuals would provide stronger evidence but are impractical at scale.\n486\t\n487\t\\textbf{Sample sizes.} Most experiments use $n{=}100$ source texts. While the $n{=}500$ replication confirms robustness for the strongest combination, other judge$\\times$benchmark combinations may be underpowered.\n488\t\n489\t\\textbf{MT-Bench null results.} CSD fails to detect any style bias on MT-Bench, which may reflect either genuine absence of bias or method insensitivity to multi-turn evaluation contexts.\n490\t\n491\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n492\t\\section{Conclusion}\n493\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n494\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n495\t\n496\tWe introduced Causal Style Decomposition, a black-box method for detecting per-axis causal style bias in LLM judges through counterfactual pairwise comparison.\n497\tOur primary contribution is methodological: standard Likert scoring is approximately $5{\\times}$ less sample-efficient than pairwise comparison for detecting moderate style bias, and can produce wrong-direction estimates.\n498\tThis measurement insight is actionable---researchers should prefer pairwise comparison when auditing LLM judges for style confounds.\n499\t\n500\tCSD detects significant formality bias on two of three frontier judges, with effects that survive original-preference correction on LLMBar and replicate across an independent rewriter.\n501\tStyle bias is axis-dependent, with a gradient from clear (formality) through borderline (register) to marginal (tone), suggesting judges are biased along a latent prestige dimension rather than uniformly across all style properties.\n502\t\n503\tThese findings highlight both the promise and current limitations of causal style auditing for LLM evaluation systems.\n504\t\n505\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n506\t% References\n507\t%--------------------------------------------------------------\n508\t\\begin{thebibliography}{20}\n509\t\n510\t\\bibitem[Ouyang et~al.(2022)]{ouyang2022training}\n511\tLong Ouyang, Jeffrey Wu, Xu Jiang, Diogo Almeida, Carroll Wainwright, Pamela Mishkin, Chong Zhang, Sandhini Agarwal, Katarina Slama, Alex Ray, et~al.\n512\t\\newblock Training language models to follow instructions with human feedback.\n513\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2022.\n514\t\n515\t\\bibitem[Dubois et~al.(2024)]{dubois2024length}\n516\tYann Dubois, Bal\\'{a}zs Galambosi, Percy Liang, and Tatsunori~B. Hashimoto.\n517\t\\newblock Length-controlled {A}lpaca{E}val: A simple way to debias automatic evaluators.\n518\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2025.\n519\t\n520\t\\bibitem[Zheng et~al.(2024)]{zheng2024judging}\n521\tLianmin Zheng, Wei-Lin Chiang, Ying Sheng, Siyuan Zhuang, Zhanghao Wu, Yonghao Zhuang, Zi Lin, Zhuohan Li, Dacheng Li, Eric~P. Xing, et~al.\n522\t\\newblock Judging {LLM}-as-a-judge with {MT-Bench} and {Chatbot Arena}.\n523\t\\newblock In \\emph{NeurIPS}, 2024.\n524\t\n525\t\\bibitem[SOS-Bench(2025)]{sosbench2025}\n526\tAnonymous.\n527\t\\newblock {SOS-Bench}: Systematic evaluation of style bias in {LLM} judges.\n528\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2025.\n529\t\n530\t\\bibitem[Raina et~al.(2024)]{raina2024llm}\n531\tVatsal Raina, Adian Liusie, and Mark~JF Gales.\n532\t\\newblock {LLM} evaluators recognize and favor their own generations.\n533\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2404.13076}, 2024.\n534\t\n535\t\\bibitem[Preference Leakage(2026)]{preferenceleakage2026}\n536\tAnonymous.\n537\t\\newblock Preference leakage: How training data biases propagate to {LLM} judges.\n538\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2026.\n539\t\n540\t\\bibitem[CASSE(2026)]{casse2026}\n541\tAnonymous.\n542\t\\newblock {CASSE}: Comprehensive assessment of style sensitivity in evaluation.\n543\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2026.\n544\t\n545\t\\bibitem[CausalBench(2025)]{causalbench2025}\n546\tAnonymous.\n547\t\\newblock {CausalBench}: Benchmarking with interventional data.\n548\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint}, 2025.\n549\t\n550\t\\bibitem[Lu et~al.(2020)]{lu2020gender}\n551\tKaiji Lu, Piotr Mardziel, Fangjing Wu, Preetam Amancharla, and Anupam Datta.\n552\t\\newblock Gender bias in neural natural language processing.\n553\t\\newblock In \\emph{Logic, Language, and Security}, 2020.\n554\t\n555\t\\bibitem[Kiritchenko and Mohammad(2017)]{kiritchenko2017best}\n556\tSvetlana Kiritchenko and Saif~M. Mohammad.\n557\t\\newblock Best-worst scaling more reliable than rating scales: A case study on sentiment intensity annotation.\n558\t\\newblock In \\emph{ACL}, 2017.\n559\t\n560\t\\bibitem[Zeng et~al.(2024)]{zeng2024llmbar}\n561\tZhiyuan Zeng, Jiatong Yu, Tianyu Gao, Yu Meng, Tanya Goyal, and Danqi Chen.\n562\t\\newblock Evaluating large language models at evaluating instruction following.\n563\t\\newblock In \\emph{ICLR}, 2024.\n564\t\n565\t\\end{thebibliography}\n566\t\n567\t\\end{document}\n568\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01UVQZASgaQxEhE3guq8rFgm","session_id":"6cdc1f76-5d9c-4713-b8a5-8974082b9307","uuid":"09c89401-e1d1-47e7-b17c-f00497f6865b","timestamp":"2026-05-20T22:04:20.849Z"}
