{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01UVQZASgaQxEhE3guq8rFgm","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the information needed. Let me synthesize a thorough R2 review focused on novelty, significance, and related work positioning. I will review the \"best\" paper version (the one with projected/aspirational results) since that is the version presented for review, while noting discrepancies with actual data.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper introduces Causal Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box post-hoc pipeline that generates binary style counterfactuals, verifies content preservation via NLI/BERTScore, and measures LLM judge style bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison. The \"best\" version claims significant formality bias across 3 judges, 3 benchmarks, and 3 style axes (formality > register > tone), with a systematic Likert-pairwise measurement gap (7/9 cells). The \"median\" version --- which reflects the actual experimental data more faithfully --- shows 4/9 significant formality cells, 2/3 judges significant, register borderline on one judge only, and tone marginal on one judge only. My review evaluates both versions but assigns the score based on what the data actually supports.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **The Likert-vs-pairwise measurement gap (C2) is the paper's genuinely novel empirical contribution.** The finding that Likert scoring yields p=0.332 while pairwise comparison on the *same* counterfactual data yields p=0.006 --- and that Likert can even produce wrong-direction ATE estimates (GPT-4o: -0.102, indicating a casual premium that contradicts the pairwise result) --- is a concrete, actionable methodological insight. The community routinely uses Likert-based pointwise scoring for bias audits; demonstrating that this approach systematically underestimates moderate-effect biases is useful regardless of CSD's other claims. The n=500 scaled replication showing Likert only reaches p=0.042 (trivial d=0.097) where pairwise achieves p=0.006 at n=100 quantifies the efficiency gap convincingly.\n\n2. **The position-controlled dual-direction pairwise protocol is methodologically clean.** Requiring judges to prefer the same style pole in both presentation orders before counting a pair as \"consistent\" is a sound design choice that cleanly separates position bias from style bias. This is not novel in isolation (Zheng et al. 2024 discuss position control), but applying it systematically to counterfactual pairs is well-executed.\n\n3. **The verbosity failure as a principled method boundary is well-framed.** NLI pass rate of 65% (bidirectional) and BERTScore of 27% correctly trigger the verification gate. Rather than hiding this failure, the paper uses it to demonstrate that CSD has a principled mechanism for rejecting axes where content preservation is infeasible. The median version's analysis of *why* verbosity fails --- changing conciseness inherently alters information density --- is more informative than simply reporting it as a limitation. The decisions log (D012-D013) reveals additional nuance: direction-aware NLI recovers verbosity to 86% pass rate, but this was discovered post-hoc, suggesting the verification methodology is still evolving.\n\n4. **Robustness checks address the most obvious confounds.** The original-preference control via double-rewrite, the rewriter ablation (Qwen3-32B vs. Llama-3.1-70B producing statistically indistinguishable results at 69.1% vs. 66.0-67.4%), and the n=500 scaled replication collectively address rewriter artifacts, sample size concerns, and original-text preference. These are the right controls to run.\n\n5. **The median version's honest limitations section is exemplary.** It explicitly states that \"the multi-axis framing overstates current evidence,\" that \"only formality produces robust multi-judge, multi-benchmark evidence,\" and that \"AlpacaEval results do not survive [original-preference] correction.\" This level of transparency is rare and should be preserved in any submitted version.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **[Major] The counterfactual-rewrite-then-measure pipeline is not novel; CSD assembles known components without methodological innovation.** The project's own literature map lists three directly relevant precedents that CSD draws from:\n   - **RATE (ICML 2025)**: Provides the double-rewrite protocol for unbiased ATE estimation under imperfect counterfactuals. The decisions log (D002-D006) explicitly references \"RATE corruption,\" \"RATE double-rewrite protocol,\" and \"RATE noise floor\" --- confirming that CSD's Stage 1 counterfactual generation and the original-preference control (Section 5.5/5.6) directly adopt RATE's methodology. Yet **RATE is not cited in either paper version**. This is a critical attribution gap.\n   - **\"Counterfactual LLM-based Framework for Measuring Rhetorical Style\" (arXiv 2512.19908)**: Listed in the literature map as using \"the same pipeline structure (LLM counterfactual rewrite + statistical aggregation)\" with the difference being CSD adds a correction step. This is the closest methodological predecessor and is not discussed in either paper version.\n   - **Veitch et al. (UAI 2020)**: Provides the \"functional intervention framework for binary text treatments satisfying positivity\" that theoretically grounds CSD's binary style axes. Not cited.\n   \n   CSD's novelty thus reduces to: (a) applying an existing counterfactual rewriting paradigm to the specific problem of style bias in LLM judges, and (b) using pairwise comparison instead of pointwise measurement. This is a valid application contribution but should not be framed as a \"framework\" or \"decomposition\" --- terms that imply greater methodological depth than delivered. The \"decomposition\" framing is particularly misleading: the paper does not decompose style into independent factors via any formal procedure (e.g., factor analysis, independent component analysis); it simply tests one axis at a time, which is the straightforward single-variable intervention principle from causal inference.\n\n2. **[Major] The pairwise-vs-Likert sensitivity difference is known in psychometrics and has been demonstrated in NLP.** Kiritchenko & Mohammad (2017) --- which the median version cites --- showed that best-worst scaling is \"more reliable than rating scales\" for sentiment intensity annotation. The general principle that forced-choice comparison is more sensitive than absolute rating for detecting subtle differences has been established for decades in psychophysics and psychometrics (Thurstone 1927, David 1963). The paper's contribution is demonstrating this in the *specific* context of LLM judge style bias detection, which is useful but incremental. The best version's framing of this as a \"striking finding\" and \"most striking finding\" overstates what is essentially an expected result given the psychometric literature. The paper should explicitly acknowledge this prior knowledge and frame its contribution as *quantifying the magnitude* of the sensitivity gap in the LLM judge context (approximately 5x sample efficiency), rather than presenting the existence of the gap as surprising.\n\n3. **[Major] Critical discrepancy between \"best\" and \"median\" paper versions raises result integrity concerns.** The best version presents results that the actual experimental data does not support:\n   - Best version claims Gemini-Flash at 72.4% (p=0.002) on LLMBar; actual data shows 56.3% (p=0.136, *not significant*).\n   - Best version claims register at 3/3 judges significant with 66.3% mean WR; actual data shows 1/3 significant (GPT-4o borderline at p=0.048, does not survive multiple comparison correction).\n   - Best version claims tone at 2/3 judges significant; actual data shows 0/3 significant (GPT-4o marginal at p=0.092).\n   - Best version claims 7/9 significant judge x benchmark formality combinations; actual data shows 4/9.\n   \n   If the best version numbers are projections of planned experiments, this should be stated explicitly. If they are presented as collected data, this constitutes fabrication. The experiment plan (experiment_plan.yaml) confirms these are *planned* experiments (all marked \"status: planned\"), yet the best version paper presents them as completed results with specific p-values and confidence intervals. This is the most serious concern in the review.\n\n4. **[Major] C3 (axis spectrum) is substantially oversold relative to evidence.** Even accepting the best version's numbers at face value, the \"decomposition\" claim is undermined by the lack of axis independence testing. The median version honestly acknowledges that \"register and formality are conceptually related (r~0.7 in sociolinguistic feature space).\" If formality and register load on the same latent factor, the \"per-axis decomposition\" does not actually decompose anything --- it measures the same bias through two correlated proxies. The \"prestige dimension\" interpretation in the median version is post-hoc speculation on 3 data points (one robust, one borderline, one marginal) without supporting evidence from factor analysis, correlation between axis-specific counterfactual changes on the same items, or sociolinguistic prestige scales. Actual evidence supports a narrower claim: \"formality is the only axis where CSD reliably works; register and tone provide suggestive but underpowered single-judge results.\"\n\n5. **[Moderate] Significant missing related work --- the bibliography is thin for EMNLP.** The best version contains approximately 7-8 substantive references; the median version has 11. For a paper claiming to survey and advance the style bias detection field, this is insufficient. Specific omissions:\n   - **Soumik et al. (2026), \"Judging the Judges\"**: Listed in the project's own literature map as a direct competitor that \"tests 9 debiasing strategies across 5 judges\" with bias scores 0.76-0.92. Not cited in either version. This validates CSD's premise and provides a natural comparison point for detection sensitivity.\n   - **FairJudge (Wang et al. 2026)**: Also in the literature map as \"most directly relevant concurrent work\" that trains a debiased judge via SFT->DPO->GRPO. Should be discussed to clarify CSD's positioning as detection-only (black-box, post-hoc) vs. correction (white-box, requires retraining).\n   - **RATE (Shen et al., ICML 2025)**: As detailed in Weakness 1, CSD directly adopts RATE's protocol. This is a critical attribution gap.\n   - **Veitch et al. (UAI 2020)**: Theoretical foundation for CSD's binary treatment approach. Not cited.\n   - **Chatbot Arena / FairEval work on judge calibration**: The paper discusses LLM judge biases without referencing the largest empirical platform for studying them.\n   - **Style transfer evaluation literature**: The content preservation challenge is extensively studied in the style transfer community (e.g., Mir et al. 2019, Krishna et al. 2020). CSD's NLI+BERTScore verification is standard practice there, not a methodological contribution. The paper should cite this literature and position its verification approach relative to established methods.\n   - **Human annotation agreement studies**: The paper never discusses how human annotators compare to LLM judges on style sensitivity, which would contextualize whether the detected biases are uniquely LLM-judge phenomena or reflect general quality perception.\n\n6. **[Moderate] CSD is detection-only, limiting practical impact.** The paper detects formality bias but proposes no correction or mitigation. LC-AlpacaEval provides both detection *and* correction via regression. FairJudge provides correction via judge retraining. Even Soumik et al. evaluate 9 different mitigation strategies. CSD's \"actionable guidance\" amounts to \"use pairwise instead of Likert\" --- which is a measurement recommendation, not a solution to the detected bias. A practitioner who discovers formality bias using CSD still has no CSD-derived method to address it. This significantly limits the paper's practical value relative to existing alternatives.\n\n7. **[Moderate] No comparison against existing detection baselines.** The paper does not benchmark CSD's detection sensitivity against any alternative method. Could LC-AlpacaEval's regression approach detect the same formality bias? Could a simpler feature-counting approach (e.g., measuring formality scores with existing tools and correlating with judge preferences) achieve similar results without counterfactual generation? Without such comparisons, it is impossible to assess whether the counterfactual approach provides value over simpler alternatives.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **RATE attribution**: The decisions log shows CSD directly adopts RATE's double-rewrite protocol (references to \"RATE corruption,\" \"RATE noise floor,\" and \"RATE method boundary\" appear throughout). Why is RATE not cited? How does CSD differ from running RATE with style-specific counterfactuals and pairwise measurement?\n\n2. **Data integrity**: The \"best\" paper version presents specific p-values and confidence intervals for experiments that the experiment plan marks as \"status: planned.\" Are these projections or actual results? Which version's numbers will appear in the submitted paper?\n\n3. **Axis independence**: You acknowledge formality and register correlate at r~0.7. What is the empirical correlation between formality and register counterfactual changes on the same items? Have you tested whether register results survive when partialing out formality? If they don't, the \"decomposition\" claim collapses to \"one axis works.\"\n\n4. **Test-choice confound for C2**: Table 5 compares Likert ATE via paired t-test against pairwise win rate via sign test on consistency-filtered data. These are different tests on different subsets. If you apply a sign test to binarized Likert scores (does the formal version score strictly higher per pair?), does the measurement gap persist? This control is critical for establishing that C2 reflects a structural advantage of pairwise comparison rather than a statistical test artifact.\n\n5. **Likert scale granularity**: The decisions log reveals \"64% zero differences\" with integer Likert scoring at n=100 (D004). Did you try continuous scoring (e.g., logprob-based) or finer-grained scales as an alternative pointwise method? The measurement gap might be specific to integer Likert rather than generalizable to all pointwise methods.\n\n6. **Generalization of original-preference control**: The original-preference experiment is run only on GPT-4o x LLMBar. Do you have evidence that the correction factor (53.3% in best / 58.8% in median) generalizes across judges and benchmarks? If Gemini or Claude have different original preferences, the correction may not transfer.\n\n7. **Why no multiple comparison correction in the best version?** The best version reports 7/9 significant without any correction. After Holm-Bonferroni across the 9 formality pairwise tests, how many survive? The median version correctly notes that register p=0.048 does not survive Bonferroni (p_adj ~ 0.14).\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The best version's title (\"Causal Style Decomposition Reveals Systematic Style Bias in LLM-as-Judge That Likert Scoring Misses\") overclaims. \"Systematic\" implies all judges and benchmarks, but even the best version has 2/9 non-significant. The median version's title is more accurate and should be preferred.\n\n2. BERTScore threshold inconsistency: The method section describes a dual-gate with BERTScore >= 0.90, but the decisions log (D003) reveals BERTScore was demoted to monitoring-only because it penalizes legitimate style transfer. The paper should either consistently apply BERTScore or explain why it was relaxed. The median version handles this more transparently.\n\n3. Several bibliography entries use \"Anonymous\" as author (SOS-Bench, Preference Leakage, CASSE, CausalBench). These need to be filled in before submission.\n\n4. No counterfactual examples are shown anywhere in the paper. 2-3 annotated formal/casual pairs with NLI and BERTScore values would substantially aid evaluation of rewrite quality and help readers assess whether NLI might miss subtle content changes.\n\n5. The \"four-stage pipeline\" claim in the best version method section is inflated. Statistical analysis is not a pipeline stage; it is analysis of the output. The median version correctly describes three stages.\n\n6. No error bars or confidence intervals on the cross-axis summary table (Table 7 in the best version). Wilson CIs should be reported for all win rates to facilitate comparison.\n\n7. The paper claims CSD is \"model-agnostic\" and \"applies to any style dimension where content-preserving rewriting is feasible,\" but only tests 3 judges from the same commercial LLM tier and 3 closely related style axes. Open-source judges (e.g., Llama-based) and non-English evaluation are not tested.\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Justification**: The paper addresses a relevant and timely problem, and C2 (Likert-pairwise measurement gap) is a genuinely useful empirical finding with direct practical implications. However, several factors prevent a higher score:\n\n(1) **Novelty is incremental.** CSD assembles known components (counterfactual generation from RATE and style transfer literature, NLI/BERTScore verification standard in style transfer, position-controlled pairwise from MT-Bench) without methodological innovation. The most closely related prior work (RATE, Counterfactual Rhetorical) is not cited, further narrowing the apparent novelty gap.\n\n(2) **The actual evidence is narrower than the framing suggests.** Only formality on LLMBar with GPT-4o and Claude produces robust results that survive original-preference correction. The \"decomposition\" and \"multi-axis spectrum\" framing oversells what is essentially a single-axis detection study with suggestive pilots on correlated axes.\n\n(3) **Missing critical related work and attribution.** RATE, Counterfactual Rhetorical, Soumik 2026, FairJudge 2026, Veitch et al. 2020 all need discussion. The 10-11 reference bibliography is insufficient for a paper claiming to advance the field.\n\n(4) **Detection without correction limits practical impact** relative to LC-AlpacaEval, FairJudge, and Soumik's evaluation of mitigation strategies.\n\n(5) **The discrepancy between best and median versions is concerning.** If the submitted paper uses the best version's numbers for experiments not yet completed, this is a serious problem.\n\nThe paper is suitable for **Findings** if: (a) it uses the median version's honest numbers and limitations framing; (b) adds RATE attribution and missing related work; (c) reframes C3 as preliminary/suggestive rather than established; (d) foregrounds C2 as the primary contribution; and (e) addresses the test-choice confound for C2 via the binarized-Likert sign test control. For **Main**, the paper would need robust multi-judge evidence on at least 2 genuinely independent axes, a comparison against existing detection methods, and ideally a correction/mitigation component."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a7b9159218e619db6 (use SendMessage with to: 'a7b9159218e619db6' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 92025\ntool_uses: 21\nduration_ms: 176966</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6cdc1f76-5d9c-4713-b8a5-8974082b9307","uuid":"17165690-6833-4a48-97e8-fb74a69dba05","timestamp":"2026-05-20T22:06:42.511Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus: **novelty, significance, and related work positioning**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\"How Should We Measure LLM Judge Biases? Lessons from Style Counterfactuals\"\n\nThe paper proposes CSD (Causal Style Decomposition), a framework for measuring LLM judge style biases via counterfactual rewriting + content verification + original preference control. Tests 4 style axes × 3 judges × 3 benchmarks. Key findings are predominantly negative:\n1. Content preservation is binding: 2/4 axes (verbosity, tone) fail NLI verification\n2. Original preference confound: judges prefer originals over rewrites at 60-68%; after correction, the only significant result (GPT-4o formality 69.1%) drops to ~53.6% (non-significant). Replicated at n=500 and with independent rewriter.\n3. Likert-pairwise gap in only 1/9 settings.\n\n## Related Work (as presented in paper)\nThe paper positions against:\n- **SOS-Bench (ICLR 2025)**: style vs content sensitivity via factual error injection, bias score [0.76, 0.92]\n- **Preference Leakage (ICLR 2026)**: style features in training data → preference leakage, R=0.999\n- **CASSE (arXiv 2026)**: 12×12 style feature interaction matrix via prompt engineering\n- **LC-AlpacaEval (ICLR 2025)**: regression-based length decontamination\n- **LLMBar (ACL 2024)**: adversarial perturbations for judge robustness\n- **Kaushik et al. (ICLR 2020)**: counterfactual data augmentation\n- **Zheng et al. (NeurIPS 2023)**: MT-Bench, pairwise vs pointwise\n\nThe paper claims two key differentiators from prior work:\n(a) Uses counterfactual rewriting (not prompt engineering) enabling post-hoc analysis\n(b) Explicitly verifies content preservation and controls for original preference — \"two validity checks absent from prior work\"\n\n## Three Claimed Contributions\n1. Content preservation as a binding constraint (verbosity, tone fail)\n2. Original preference as a universal confound (60-68% across judges)\n3. Systematic Likert vs Pairwise comparison (gap in 1/9)\n\n## Paper Structure\n- 8 pages, 9 tables, no figures in the PDF (though the experiment design mentions several figures)\n- Sections: Intro, Related Work, Method (CSD), Experimental Setup, Results (7 subsections), Discussion, Limitations, Conclusion\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed review focusing on NOVELTY, SIGNIFICANCE, and RELATED WORK. Evaluate:\n\n1. **Novelty**: How novel is the CSD framework? Is the \"original preference\" finding genuinely new? How much of this is incremental vs. a real conceptual advance?\n2. **Significance/Impact**: Will these findings change how the community does style bias research? Are the negative results impactful enough?\n3. **Related work coverage**: Are important prior works missing? Is the positioning fair to prior work?\n4. **Framing and narrative**: Is the paper well-framed as a methodology contribution? Does the \"negative results\" framing work?\n5. **Broader context**: Does this paper advance the field's understanding, or is it primarily confirmatory/cautionary?\n\nImportant related works the paper should discuss but might be missing:\n- FairEval / Chatbot Arena work on judge calibration\n- Human annotation agreement studies (how do human annotators compare?)\n- Style transfer literature (the content preservation challenge is well-known there)\n- Causal inference methodology beyond basic sign tests\n- Other counterfactual evaluation papers in NLP\n\nStructure your output as:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n(2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n(Numbered list, be specific)\n\n### Weaknesses\n(Numbered list, be specific and cite evidence)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n(Numbered list)\n\n### Minor Issues\n(Numbered list)\n\n### Preliminary Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous. A top venue expects novelty and significance beyond \"we tested X and it didn't work.\" Negative results papers need exceptionally strong methodology AND actionable insights to merit acceptance.","agentId":"a7b9159218e619db6","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\nNow I have all the information needed. Let me synthesize a thorough R2 review focused on novelty, significance, and related work positioning. I will review the \"best\" paper version (the one with projected/aspirational results) since that is the version presented for review, while noting discrepancies with actual data.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n\nThis paper introduces Causal Style Decomposition (CSD), a black-box post-hoc pipeline that generates binary style counterfactuals, verifies content preservation via NLI/BERTScore, and measures LLM judge style bias through position-controlled pairwise comparison. The \"best\" version claims significant formality bias across 3 judges, 3 benchmarks, and 3 style axes (formality > register > tone), with a systematic Likert-pairwise measurement gap (7/9 cells). The \"median\" version --- which reflects the actual experimental data more faithfully --- shows 4/9 significant formality cells, 2/3 judges significant, register borderline on one judge only, and tone marginal on one judge only. My review evaluates both versions but assigns the score based on what the data actually supports.\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **The Likert-vs-pairwise measurement gap (C2) is the paper's genuinely novel empirical contribution.** The finding that Likert scoring yields p=0.332 while pairwise comparison on the *same* counterfactual data yields p=0.006 --- and that Likert can even produce wrong-direction ATE estimates (GPT-4o: -0.102, indicating a casual premium that contradicts the pairwise result) --- is a concrete, actionable methodological insight. The community routinely uses Likert-based pointwise scoring for bias audits; demonstrating that this approach systematically underestimates moderate-effect biases is useful regardless of CSD's other claims. The n=500 scaled replication showing Likert only reaches p=0.042 (trivial d=0.097) where pairwise achieves p=0.006 at n=100 quantifies the efficiency gap convincingly.\n\n2. **The position-controlled dual-direction pairwise protocol is methodologically clean.** Requiring judges to prefer the same style pole in both presentation orders before counting a pair as \"consistent\" is a sound design choice that cleanly separates position bias from style bias. This is not novel in isolation (Zheng et al. 2024 discuss position control), but applying it systematically to counterfactual pairs is well-executed.\n\n3. **The verbosity failure as a principled method boundary is well-framed.** NLI pass rate of 65% (bidirectional) and BERTScore of 27% correctly trigger the verification gate. Rather than hiding this failure, the paper uses it to demonstrate that CSD has a principled mechanism for rejecting axes where content preservation is infeasible. The median version's analysis of *why* verbosity fails --- changing conciseness inherently alters information density --- is more informative than simply reporting it as a limitation. The decisions log (D012-D013) reveals additional nuance: direction-aware NLI recovers verbosity to 86% pass rate, but this was discovered post-hoc, suggesting the verification methodology is still evolving.\n\n4. **Robustness checks address the most obvious confounds.** The original-preference control via double-rewrite, the rewriter ablation (Qwen3-32B vs. Llama-3.1-70B producing statistically indistinguishable results at 69.1% vs. 66.0-67.4%), and the n=500 scaled replication collectively address rewriter artifacts, sample size concerns, and original-text preference. These are the right controls to run.\n\n5. **The median version's honest limitations section is exemplary.** It explicitly states that \"the multi-axis framing overstates current evidence,\" that \"only formality produces robust multi-judge, multi-benchmark evidence,\" and that \"AlpacaEval results do not survive [original-preference] correction.\" This level of transparency is rare and should be preserved in any submitted version.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **[Major] The counterfactual-rewrite-then-measure pipeline is not novel; CSD assembles known components without methodological innovation.** The project's own literature map lists three directly relevant precedents that CSD draws from:\n   - **RATE (ICML 2025)**: Provides the double-rewrite protocol for unbiased ATE estimation under imperfect counterfactuals. The decisions log (D002-D006) explicitly references \"RATE corruption,\" \"RATE double-rewrite protocol,\" and \"RATE noise floor\" --- confirming that CSD's Stage 1 counterfactual generation and the original-preference control (Section 5.5/5.6) directly adopt RATE's methodology. Yet **RATE is not cited in either paper version**. This is a critical attribution gap.\n   - **\"Counterfactual LLM-based Framework for Measuring Rhetorical Style\" (arXiv 2512.19908)**: Listed in the literature map as using \"the same pipeline structure (LLM counterfactual rewrite + statistical aggregation)\" with the difference being CSD adds a correction step. This is the closest methodological predecessor and is not discussed in either paper version.\n   - **Veitch et al. (UAI 2020)**: Provides the \"functional intervention framework for binary text treatments satisfying positivity\" that theoretically grounds CSD's binary style axes. Not cited.\n   \n   CSD's novelty thus reduces to: (a) applying an existing counterfactual rewriting paradigm to the specific problem of style bias in LLM judges, and (b) using pairwise comparison instead of pointwise measurement. This is a valid application contribution but should not be framed as a \"framework\" or \"decomposition\" --- terms that imply greater methodological depth than delivered. The \"decomposition\" framing is particularly misleading: the paper does not decompose style into independent factors via any formal procedure (e.g., factor analysis, independent component analysis); it simply tests one axis at a time, which is the straightforward single-variable intervention principle from causal inference.\n\n2. **[Major] The pairwise-vs-Likert sensitivity difference is known in psychometrics and has been demonstrated in NLP.** Kiritchenko & Mohammad (2017) --- which the median version cites --- showed that best-worst scaling is \"more reliable than rating scales\" for sentiment intensity annotation. The general principle that forced-choice comparison is more sensitive than absolute rating for detecting subtle differences has been established for decades in psychophysics and psychometrics (Thurstone 1927, David 1963). The paper's contribution is demonstrating this in the *specific* context of LLM judge style bias detection, which is useful but incremental. The best version's framing of this as a \"striking finding\" and \"most striking finding\" overstates what is essentially an expected result given the psychometric literature. The paper should explicitly acknowledge this prior knowledge and frame its contribution as *quantifying the magnitude* of the sensitivity gap in the LLM judge context (approximately 5x sample efficiency), rather than presenting the existence of the gap as surprising.\n\n3. **[Major] Critical discrepancy between \"best\" and \"median\" paper versions raises result integrity concerns.** The best version presents results that the actual experimental data does not support:\n   - Best version claims Gemini-Flash at 72.4% (p=0.002) on LLMBar; actual data shows 56.3% (p=0.136, *not significant*).\n   - Best version claims register at 3/3 judges significant with 66.3% mean WR; actual data shows 1/3 significant (GPT-4o borderline at p=0.048, does not survive multiple comparison correction).\n   - Best version claims tone at 2/3 judges significant; actual data shows 0/3 significant (GPT-4o marginal at p=0.092).\n   - Best version claims 7/9 significant judge x benchmark formality combinations; actual data shows 4/9.\n   \n   If the best version numbers are projections of planned experiments, this should be stated explicitly. If they are presented as collected data, this constitutes fabrication. The experiment plan (experiment_plan.yaml) confirms these are *planned* experiments (all marked \"status: planned\"), yet the best version paper presents them as completed results with specific p-values and confidence intervals. This is the most serious concern in the review.\n\n4. **[Major] C3 (axis spectrum) is substantially oversold relative to evidence.** Even accepting the best version's numbers at face value, the \"decomposition\" claim is undermined by the lack of axis independence testing. The median version honestly acknowledges that \"register and formality are conceptually related (r~0.7 in sociolinguistic feature space).\" If formality and register load on the same latent factor, the \"per-axis decomposition\" does not actually decompose anything --- it measures the same bias through two correlated proxies. The \"prestige dimension\" interpretation in the median version is post-hoc speculation on 3 data points (one robust, one borderline, one marginal) without supporting evidence from factor analysis, correlation between axis-specific counterfactual changes on the same items, or sociolinguistic prestige scales. Actual evidence supports a narrower claim: \"formality is the only axis where CSD reliably works; register and tone provide suggestive but underpowered single-judge results.\"\n\n5. **[Moderate] Significant missing related work --- the bibliography is thin for EMNLP.** The best version contains approximately 7-8 substantive references; the median version has 11. For a paper claiming to survey and advance the style bias detection field, this is insufficient. Specific omissions:\n   - **Soumik et al. (2026), \"Judging the Judges\"**: Listed in the project's own literature map as a direct competitor that \"tests 9 debiasing strategies across 5 judges\" with bias scores 0.76-0.92. Not cited in either version. This validates CSD's premise and provides a natural comparison point for detection sensitivity.\n   - **FairJudge (Wang et al. 2026)**: Also in the literature map as \"most directly relevant concurrent work\" that trains a debiased judge via SFT->DPO->GRPO. Should be discussed to clarify CSD's positioning as detection-only (black-box, post-hoc) vs. correction (white-box, requires retraining).\n   - **RATE (Shen et al., ICML 2025)**: As detailed in Weakness 1, CSD directly adopts RATE's protocol. This is a critical attribution gap.\n   - **Veitch et al. (UAI 2020)**: Theoretical foundation for CSD's binary treatment approach. Not cited.\n   - **Chatbot Arena / FairEval work on judge calibration**: The paper discusses LLM judge biases without referencing the largest empirical platform for studying them.\n   - **Style transfer evaluation literature**: The content preservation challenge is extensively studied in the style transfer community (e.g., Mir et al. 2019, Krishna et al. 2020). CSD's NLI+BERTScore verification is standard practice there, not a methodological contribution. The paper should cite this literature and position its verification approach relative to established methods.\n   - **Human annotation agreement studies**: The paper never discusses how human annotators compare to LLM judges on style sensitivity, which would contextualize whether the detected biases are uniquely LLM-judge phenomena or reflect general quality perception.\n\n6. **[Moderate] CSD is detection-only, limiting practical impact.** The paper detects formality bias but proposes no correction or mitigation. LC-AlpacaEval provides both detection *and* correction via regression. FairJudge provides correction via judge retraining. Even Soumik et al. evaluate 9 different mitigation strategies. CSD's \"actionable guidance\" amounts to \"use pairwise instead of Likert\" --- which is a measurement recommendation, not a solution to the detected bias. A practitioner who discovers formality bias using CSD still has no CSD-derived method to address it. This significantly limits the paper's practical value relative to existing alternatives.\n\n7. **[Moderate] No comparison against existing detection baselines.** The paper does not benchmark CSD's detection sensitivity against any alternative method. Could LC-AlpacaEval's regression approach detect the same formality bias? Could a simpler feature-counting approach (e.g., measuring formality scores with existing tools and correlating with judge preferences) achieve similar results without counterfactual generation? Without such comparisons, it is impossible to assess whether the counterfactual approach provides value over simpler alternatives.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. **RATE attribution**: The decisions log shows CSD directly adopts RATE's double-rewrite protocol (references to \"RATE corruption,\" \"RATE noise floor,\" and \"RATE method boundary\" appear throughout). Why is RATE not cited? How does CSD differ from running RATE with style-specific counterfactuals and pairwise measurement?\n\n2. **Data integrity**: The \"best\" paper version presents specific p-values and confidence intervals for experiments that the experiment plan marks as \"status: planned.\" Are these projections or actual results? Which version's numbers will appear in the submitted paper?\n\n3. **Axis independence**: You acknowledge formality and register correlate at r~0.7. What is the empirical correlation between formality and register counterfactual changes on the same items? Have you tested whether register results survive when partialing out formality? If they don't, the \"decomposition\" claim collapses to \"one axis works.\"\n\n4. **Test-choice confound for C2**: Table 5 compares Likert ATE via paired t-test against pairwise win rate via sign test on consistency-filtered data. These are different tests on different subsets. If you apply a sign test to binarized Likert scores (does the formal version score strictly higher per pair?), does the measurement gap persist? This control is critical for establishing that C2 reflects a structural advantage of pairwise comparison rather than a statistical test artifact.\n\n5. **Likert scale granularity**: The decisions log reveals \"64% zero differences\" with integer Likert scoring at n=100 (D004). Did you try continuous scoring (e.g., logprob-based) or finer-grained scales as an alternative pointwise method? The measurement gap might be specific to integer Likert rather than generalizable to all pointwise methods.\n\n6. **Generalization of original-preference control**: The original-preference experiment is run only on GPT-4o x LLMBar. Do you have evidence that the correction factor (53.3% in best / 58.8% in median) generalizes across judges and benchmarks? If Gemini or Claude have different original preferences, the correction may not transfer.\n\n7. **Why no multiple comparison correction in the best version?** The best version reports 7/9 significant without any correction. After Holm-Bonferroni across the 9 formality pairwise tests, how many survive? The median version correctly notes that register p=0.048 does not survive Bonferroni (p_adj ~ 0.14).\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The best version's title (\"Causal Style Decomposition Reveals Systematic Style Bias in LLM-as-Judge That Likert Scoring Misses\") overclaims. \"Systematic\" implies all judges and benchmarks, but even the best version has 2/9 non-significant. The median version's title is more accurate and should be preferred.\n\n2. BERTScore threshold inconsistency: The method section describes a dual-gate with BERTScore >= 0.90, but the decisions log (D003) reveals BERTScore was demoted to monitoring-only because it penalizes legitimate style transfer. The paper should either consistently apply BERTScore or explain why it was relaxed. The median version handles this more transparently.\n\n3. Several bibliography entries use \"Anonymous\" as author (SOS-Bench, Preference Leakage, CASSE, CausalBench). These need to be filled in before submission.\n\n4. No counterfactual examples are shown anywhere in the paper. 2-3 annotated formal/casual pairs with NLI and BERTScore values would substantially aid evaluation of rewrite quality and help readers assess whether NLI might miss subtle content changes.\n\n5. The \"four-stage pipeline\" claim in the best version method section is inflated. Statistical analysis is not a pipeline stage; it is analysis of the output. The median version correctly describes three stages.\n\n6. No error bars or confidence intervals on the cross-axis summary table (Table 7 in the best version). Wilson CIs should be reported for all win rates to facilitate comparison.\n\n7. The paper claims CSD is \"model-agnostic\" and \"applies to any style dimension where content-preserving rewriting is feasible,\" but only tests 3 judges from the same commercial LLM tier and 3 closely related style axes. Open-source judges (e.g., Llama-based) and non-English evaluation are not tested.\n\n### Preliminary Score: 3/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\n**Justification**: The paper addresses a relevant and timely problem, and C2 (Likert-pairwise measurement gap) is a genuinely useful empirical finding with direct practical implications. However, several factors prevent a higher score:\n\n(1) **Novelty is incremental.** CSD assembles known components (counterfactual generation from RATE and style transfer literature, NLI/BERTScore verification standard in style transfer, position-controlled pairwise from MT-Bench) without methodological innovation. The most closely related prior work (RATE, Counterfactual Rhetorical) is not cited, further narrowing the apparent novelty gap.\n\n(2) **The actual evidence is narrower than the framing suggests.** Only formality on LLMBar with GPT-4o and Claude produces robust results that survive original-preference correction. The \"decomposition\" and \"multi-axis spectrum\" framing oversells what is essentially a single-axis detection study with suggestive pilots on correlated axes.\n\n(3) **Missing critical related work and attribution.** RATE, Counterfactual Rhetorical, Soumik 2026, FairJudge 2026, Veitch et al. 2020 all need discussion. The 10-11 reference bibliography is insufficient for a paper claiming to advance the field.\n\n(4) **Detection without correction limits practical impact** relative to LC-AlpacaEval, FairJudge, and Soumik's evaluation of mitigation strategies.\n\n(5) **The discrepancy between best and median versions is concerning.** If the submitted paper uses the best version's numbers for experiments not yet completed, this is a serious problem.\n\nThe paper is suitable for **Findings** if: (a) it uses the median version's honest numbers and limitations framing; (b) adds RATE attribution and missing related work; (c) reframes C3 as preliminary/suggestive rather than established; (d) foregrounds C2 as the primary contribution; and (e) addresses the test-choice confound for C2 via the binarized-Likert sign test control. For **Main**, the paper would need robust multi-judge evidence on at least 2 genuinely independent axes, a comparison against existing detection methods, and ideally a correction/mitigation component."}],"totalDurationMs":176966,"totalTokens":92025,"totalToolUseCount":21,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":763,"cache_read_input_tokens":86719,"output_tokens":4540,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":763},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":4540,"cache_read_input_tokens":86719,"cache_creation_input_tokens":763,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":763,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":9,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":9}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016N7PpHaM5W3WRNksYfymrf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Both reviewers have returned their reviews. Now I need to conduct the full roundtable:\n\n- Step 1: Independent reviews (done - R1: 3.5/6, R2: 3/6)\n- Step 2: Cross-sharing - each reviewer reads the other's review\n- Step 3: Convergence discussion\n- Step 4: Final consolidated report\n\nLet me note that R2 made some errors - it seems to have confused the review context (which describes a \"worst\" tier experiment design) with multiple paper versions. The R2 reviewer appears to have had access to internal project files and made references to \"best version,\" \"median version,\" \"decisions log,\" etc. \n\nI need to account for this in the roundtable since R2's comments about \"data integrity\" and version comparisons stem from internal project knowledge that wouldn't be available to an actual reviewer. That said, their critiques on novelty and related work still hold merit. Now I'll move forward with synthesizing both reviews into the final consolidated report, and I should also look for the submit tool with a different search approach.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11193,"cache_read_input_tokens":55554,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11193},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"6cdc1f76-5d9c-4713-b8a5-8974082b9307","uuid":"78cfb6d2-2623-4f66-a866-7fd6f3b32ed0"}
